{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Four-round DIV and REM\n\nThe core now uses one unsigned restoring divider for all four RV32M divide and remainder instructions. Issue captures the forwarded operand magnitudes and the result sign. Each of the next four clocks registers eight quotient bits and the partial remainder. The result stays valid until EX/MEM accepts it. Formal ALTOPS captures its per-op substitute on the same issue edge and uses the same handshake.\n\nID/EX holds the instruction metadata while the divider runs. EX saves the forwarded source values for RVFI because their producers can retire during the wait. An older memory access can hold EX/MEM until its bus handshake. Once it advances, EX emits bubbles until the divide result transfers through the existing forwarding path. The next instruction can enter ID/EX on that transfer edge.\n\nThe arithmetic handles the exceptional results within the regular four rounds. A zero divisor yields an all-ones quotient and the dividend magnitude as remainder. Signed quotient negation is suppressed for a zero divisor. The magnitude of INT_MIN divided by the magnitude of -1 already gives the required overflow bit pattern. A guarded include in the ALU makes the requested alphabetical Verilator source glob work alongside the project's package-first builds.\n\n## Local verification\n\n| Check | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Verilator lint | Passed with both the requested source glob and the Makefile source order |\n| Fast formal, single-channel ALTOPS | 53 passed, 0 failed on the first run |\n| Cocotb | All 81 tests passed, including four-round latency, result backpressure, reset, and 68 reference operand pairs per divide/remainder opcode |\n| Pipeline regression | A stalled load fed a dependent DIV/REM/DIV sequence. RVFI reported the expected operands and consecutive orders. The bus accepted the result store exactly once while a following DIV waited behind it. |\n| Co-simulation | Selftest and three random programs matched the ISS in both bus modes. Stalled CoreMark matched all five expected CRCs and printed “Correct operation validated.” |\n\n## Performance\n\nTen iterations took **4,491,485 marker cycles**, matching the supplied baseline. The start marker was at cycle 15,801 and the stop marker at 4,507,286.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached **31.114, 31.863, and 31.863 MHz** respectively. The median of **31.863 MHz** gives **70.94 iterations/s** at the measured cycle count.\n\nThe eight-step divider round is now the timing limit in all three placements. Option 0's worst path runs from the partial-remainder register back to itself. Divide-heavy workloads incur five extra EX cycles per instruction for issue and the four rounds before transfer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
