{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'is_mul|multiplier u_mul|reg_q\\\\.alu_result <=|id_ex_wb_only' cores/bench/rtl/{core_pkg,core,hazard_unit,mem_stage}.sv; rg -n 'alu\\\\.sv|mem_stage\\\\.sv' cores/bench/test/test_*.py; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv:13:  input  logic       id_ex_wb_only,     // valid EX producer first bypassed in WB\ncores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv:39:  assign use_hazard   = id_ex_wb_only && id_ex_rd != 5'b0\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv:186:    .id_ex_wb_only  (id_ex_w.valid && (id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read || is_mul(id_ex_w.ctrl.alu_op))),\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:41:  function automatic logic is_mul(input logic [4:0] op);\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv:42:    is_mul = (op == ALU_MUL)   || (op == ALU_MULH) ||\ncores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv:54:  multiplier u_mul (\ncores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv:138:      reg_q.alu_result <= is_mul(in.ctrl.alu_op) ? mul_result : in.alu_result;\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py:297:               sources=[\"core_pkg.sv\", \"alu.sv\"],\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:593:            \"alu.sv\", \"decoder.sv\", \"imm_gen.sv\", \"reg_file.sv\",\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:595:            \"multiplier.sv\", \"mem_stage.sv\", \"wb_stage.sv\",\n# Execute multiplies in MEM\n\nThe dedicated multiplier takes the forwarded operands from EX/MEM and writes the selected low or high word into MEM/WB. It carries all four original RV32M product definitions and their ALTOPS equivalents. Data-memory addresses still come directly from the EX/MEM ALU result.\n\nThe load-use interlock now also recognizes valid multiplies. An immediate consumer of a nonzero destination waits one cycle and takes the product from WB. Independent multiplies retire on consecutive clocks. The existing MEM/WB hold retains a product needed by an instruction stalled behind an older memory access.\n\nThe implementation uses a shared opcode predicate in `core_pkg.sv` so the interlock and writeback mux recognize the same four operations. The interlock still compares both raw instruction source fields, so an immediate that aliases the destination can cause an extra bubble.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator passed the requested lint command with zero warnings.\n- All 89 cocotb tests passed. The multiplier suite covers the original exact vectors, 25 boundary pairs and 256 seeded random pairs. Pipeline tests check retirement spacing, x0, WB forwarding to a multiply, store and branch, and operand retention under bus stalls.\n- The single-retire ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks on the first run.\n- All eight RVFI comparisons passed for selftest and three random programs, each with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark printed “Correct operation validated.” and matched all five expected CRCs.\n\n## Local fitness\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took 4,238,604 marker cycles, up 0.86% from the champion. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 58.897, 61.962 and 61.962 MHz. The median gives **146.18 iterations/s**, a 22.76% gain over the champion. Placement 0 used 3,095 LUT4s, 644 registers and six DSPs. Its worst setup path still crosses the register file and EX ALU on the way to data RAM.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
