{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Decode-stage fetch prediction\n\nThe local FPGA evaluation measured **13.31 CoreMark iterations/s**, up 9.8% from the 12.12 baseline. The ten-iteration bracket with both stall generators active took 4,163,829 cycles, saving 327,656 cycles.\n\n| Local measurement | Baseline | Decode prediction |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| CoreMark bracket cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,163,829 |\n| Median Gowin Fmax | 5.442 MHz | 5.544 MHz |\n| LUT4s | 12,312 | 12,586 |\n\n## Implementation\n\nID uses the legal transfer decode and WB-bypassed register reads to evaluate all six branch conditions. One adder computes the PC-relative or JALR target. JALR clears bit zero after addition. A taken prediction requires a word-aligned target below 0x00100000.\n\nIF installs the successor on the edge that admits the instruction to ID/EX. EX uses forwarded operands to resolve the architectural successor and compares it with the registered fetch PC. A mismatch squashes the younger instruction. An older data-memory wait holds the PC and ID/EX together. On release, correction has priority over an instruction-memory wait. RVFI records the resolved successor, and a successful jump writes PC+4 as its link.\n\nI also changed the hazard unit to inject bubbles directly at ID/EX. This removes the instruction-masking mux ahead of the predictor. ID and EX use a shared SystemVerilog function for branch comparisons. A guarded include in core.sv allows the requested wildcard-order lint command to find the pipeline types.\n\n## Verification and tradeoffs\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled.\n- Single-retire ALTOPS formal passed on the first run, with 53 checks passed and zero failures.\n- All five cocotb suites passed, including 19 pipeline tests. Directed checks cover consecutive retirements, stale branch and JALR operands, misaligned and out-of-range predictions, illegal encodings, load-use stalls, overlapping bus stalls, and wrong-path store suppression.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and three random programs against the ISS, both with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark matched all five CRCs, printed the validation marker, and reported no out-of-bounds access.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 5.321, 5.544 and 5.544 MHz. The predictor costs 274 additional LUT4s. An outstanding register write can still cause a one-slot correction when the decode operands are stale.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
