{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_177","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short --ignored | head -30 && cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && date -u'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n!! .agent.last\n!! .agent.log\n!! cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n!! formal/riscv-formal\n# Load-table bank selection at EX/MEM\n\nThe load table has two 128-row distributed RAM banks. Each EX/MEM transfer captures both tags, signatures, raw words and width-selected forwarding values, along with the logical index. EX qualifies each bank with the operand's speculative-load enable. This applies to both source registers and to the register source of ALU operand B.\n\nMEM compares each bank's tag and subword format in parallel. I placed the comparisons inside each bank because selecting the raw and formatted data before a shared verifier measured 64.480 MHz median. The registered bank bit selects the applicable checks, raw RVFI word and writeback candidate. A hit requires the complete RAM word address and a legal alignment. A narrow bypass also requires agreement on width, signedness, byte offset and the raw bytes. A failed verification uses the existing replay path.\n\nThe occupancy directory samples a 16-bit group at EX/MEM and selects one bit in MEM. Reset clears the 16 group flags and invalidates the sample. An update initializes a previously unused group from zero. Accepted narrow stores clear their row, simultaneous updates suppress a lookup of the same row, and bus holds freeze the payload and directory samples together.\n\nTwo pipeline regressions cover opposite-bank forwarding at the same bank-local row, concurrent stores and lookups, and the first install and invalidation after a warm reset. They check external reads, RVFI values and the cycle gap to the dependent instruction.\n\n## Local verification and timing\n\nVerilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All six cocotb test modules passed, including 38 pipeline cases. The single-retirement ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks. Co-simulation passed selftest and three random instruction traces with both bus configurations. The stalled 10-iteration CoreMark run produced all five canonical CRCs and the validation message. The start and stop markers were at cycles 11,974 and 3,152,769, giving 3,140,795 measured cycles.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 69.307, 77.566 and 77.566 MHz. The median gives 246.96 CoreMark iterations/s. The design preserved the marker cycle count but fell short of the 81.981 MHz replacement threshold. The median placement's longest path ran from a registered bank candidate through subword verification and replay control to an ID operand register.\nMon Nov 23 05:09:11 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
