{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_161","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'print(84.703e6*10/3140795); print(87.293e6*10/3140795)' ; git diff --numstat; rg -n '\"'^(  assign div_op|  assign div_result|      reg_q.alu_result)'\"' cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"269.68649657172784\n277.93281637292466\n103\t38\tcores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n1\t0\tcores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n10\t6\tcores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n7\t2\tcores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n33\t1\tcores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n204:  assign div_op = in.valid && (in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_DIV || in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_DIVU ||\n206:  assign div_result = in.div_negate ? -in.div_magnitude : in.div_magnitude;\n220:      reg_q.alu_result <= in.alu_result | mul_result | ({32{div_op}} & div_result);\n# Divider implementation\n\nDIV and REM share fifteen trial subtractors with 290 data bits in total and one borrow bit per trial. The setup cycle selects four, three, two or one dividend bits per round at divisor-magnitude thresholds of 2¹¹, 2¹⁸ and 2²⁵. It prepares the odd multiples and the first partial dividend. Six registered rounds and a held final trial preserve the response latency.\n\nThe 28-bit stream holds six pending dividend groups and the first quotient digit after setup. Each round replaces a consumed nibble with a quotient digit. The held digits compact in parallel with the final trial, whose digit already occupies the low bits for every radix.\n\nAt the EX/MEM edge, four registers capture the masked remainder groups at widths 32, 21, 15 and 15. Two further registers capture the enabled quotient and the dividend magnitude for REM by zero. MEM combines these terms and restores the sign for WB. These registers sample every edge because a valid divide in MEM always advances. The standalone response and ALTOPS data still follow the original handshake.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed for hardware arithmetic and ALTOPS.\n- All 26 ALU and 37 pipeline tests passed. The ALU tests check both sides of each radix threshold, the highest eligible digit in every reachable position, signed operands, response holds and the registered MEM magnitude.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and three random programs, each with and without bus stalls. CoreMark validated all five CRCs for ten iterations with both stalls enabled. Its markers occurred at cycles 11,974 and 3,152,769, spanning 3,140,795 cycles.\n- The standard ALTOPS formal suite and all three Gowin placements are running.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
