{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# MEM-to-EX load forwarding\n\nLW, LH, LHU and LBU now feed either EX operand directly on the data-bus acceptance edge. Parallel lane masks select the bytes and extend the selected signed halfword. The EX/MEM register captures the forwarded base plus the immediate and holds that address throughout a stalled request. MEM qualifies forwarding with the post-alignment write enable so a trapping load preserves an older writer to the same register. Signed LB and all four multiplies retain their one-cycle WB interlock.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed datapath. Directed tests check both ADD operands and retirement spacing at every legal lane offset, two wait cycles per request in a dependent load/load/store sequence, and forwarding priority after a misaligned LW.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with no warnings.\n- All 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks passed on the first run.\n- All 92 cocotb tests passed. Selftest and three random programs matched the reference ISS with and without bus stalls, for eight RVFI trace comparisons.\n- With both buses stalled, ten CoreMark iterations took 4,035,848 cycles between markers 14,682 and 4,050,530. The UART validation message and all five canonical CRCs matched.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 64.423, 64.560 and 64.560 MHz. The median gives 159.97 iterations/s, 9.43% above the supplied 146.18 baseline. Placement 0 used 3,243 LUT4s and 676 flip-flops.\n\nThe principal timing constraint in placements 1 and 2 is the path from data RAM through load formatting, operand forwarding and the ALU to the registered EX result.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
