{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_184","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'date -u; cat /proc/loadavg\ntail -3 \"$TMPDIR/mixed-radix/formal-selected.log\"\nrg -c '\"'DONE \\\\(FAIL|ERROR:' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/mixed-radix/formal-selected.log\" || true\ncat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"Mon Nov 23 01:25:07 UTC 2026\n27.78 46.32 42.31 27/1042 4\nSBY  2:20:33 [insn_xor_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\nSBY  2:20:33 [insn_xor_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\nSBY  2:20:33 [insn_xor_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:# Mixed-radix RV32M divider\n\nThe divider resolves the two leading magnitude bits during setup, then emits a radix-4 digit and a radix-8 digit on each of six clocks. Setup registers 3D, 5D and 7D. The radix-4 stage uses a balanced borrow tree. The seven parallel radix-8 subtractions produce interval masks for the residual and three quotient bits. Division by zero selects the maximum digit at every step and passes the dividend through as the remainder.\n\nTwo details affect timing. The request edge loads the remaining 30 dividend bits directly into the upper bits of the quotient register. Setup fills its two low bits. At the start of the last round only 27 dividend bits have been consumed, so each radix-4 residual fits in 29 bits. The RTL wires the unreachable high bits of the shifted partial dividends to zero and retains full-width divisor multiples and borrow trials.\n\nThe EX result mux selects jump-link or divide data in parallel with the regular ALU. This puts one final mux on the regular ALU path and preserves jump priority and the divide handshake.\n\nThe new tests check unsigned quotient and remainder at kD−1, kD and kD+1 for k from 1 through 7 across a range of divisor widths. They also cover the preceding radix-4 transitions at quotients 8, 16 and 24, and all 20 combinations of the two-bit dividend prefix and divisor 0 through 4. The existing signed, reset, backpressure and seven-clock latency checks are retained.\n\n## Local verification\n\nVerilator lint and all six cocotb suites passed, including all 24 ALU tests. Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS with ready buses and with both buses stalled. CoreMark completed ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,207,017 marker cycles with both buses stalled.\n\nAn initial ALTOPS formal run passed all 53 checks. Yosys also proved the final EX stage equivalent to the original EX stage under ALTOPS. In the full final run, 52 of 53 checks have passed and the register-consistency check is in progress.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 68.141, 70.620 and 70.620 MHz. The 70.620 MHz median gives 220.20 iterations/s, a 6.93% gain over the 66.043 MHz champion at the same marker count. This clears the 4.6% replacement threshold. With the original EX result mux, the same divider reached 68.584 MHz median.\n\nThe median placement uses 4,824 logic cells and 1,470 registers. Its limiting path runs from the register file through EX to an instruction-fetch successor table occupancy bit.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
