{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_230","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check; git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Radix-16 division across EX and MEM\n\nThe divider captures operand magnitudes and sign control on request. Setup looks up the quotient and remainder of the four-bit prefix and registers the odd multiples through 15D. Six clocked rounds and one response-cycle round share fifteen parallel subtractions. Each subtraction has a 36-bit multiple and an independent borrow bit. The state driving the final digit holds until transfer.\n\nEX transfers the magnitude through its existing ALU-result register with a negate bit. MEM applies the sign before writeback. ID/EX holds the instruction metadata, and the request handshake saves both forwarded operands for RVFI. The standalone ALU exposes the signed result. ALTOPS uses the same handshake and result path with negation disabled.\n\nDecode marks all M-extension results as first available in WB. The forwarding units register the operand selections alongside their producers. For ALU operand B they also resolve immediate-versus-register qualification before EX. Stores and RVFI still receive the forwarded register value. The multiplier takes its first operand directly from a forwarded EX register. A separate ALU control for PC-derived results lets link, AUIPC, divide and base integer results merge in parallel.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. All 110 cocotb tests passed, including every prefix and final digit, overflowing multiples, response backpressure, reset cancellation and dependent consumers of all four division operations.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest, three random programs and CoreMark. Ten CoreMark iterations passed all five CRC checks in 3,140,795 marker cycles with instruction and data stalls.\n- The single-retire ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks on the first run for the selected RTL, including register consistency and liveness.\n\n## Timing\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 77.594, 83.455 and 83.455 MHz. The median gives 265.71 iterations/s, a 6.48% improvement over 78.376 MHz at the same cycle count. The median placement's worst path runs through the final divider trial into the EX/MEM ALU-result register. Placement option 0 falls below the 81.98 MHz replacement threshold.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_231","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented radix-16 DIV/REM across EX and MEM. Median Gowin Fmax reached **83.455 MHz**, yielding **265.71 CoreMark iterations/s**, a **6.48% gain** over the champion and above the 4.6% replacement threshold.\n\nLint, all 53 formal checks, all 110 cocotb tests, and five-program cosimulation passed. Ten CRC-validated CoreMark iterations took 3,140,795 marker cycles.\n\n[Implementation notes](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep6/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-002-r10s1/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md)"}}
