{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_180","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Split division at the 16-bit boundary\n\nThe divider classifies the signed or unsigned divisor by magnitude at acceptance. Divisors from 1 through 65,535 use fifteen parallel 20-bit trials and a 16-bit restored remainder. Larger divisors use three radix-4 trials and a 16-bit quotient. Their first trial uses the high 18 dividend bits and checks each multiple for overflow. Both arrays share the registered 3D multiple. Setup, six registered rounds and the final trial on the held state preserve the seven-clock response latency after acceptance.\n\nSetup registers mutually exclusive quotient, remainder and divide-by-zero enables. The wide remainder path reproduces the dividend magnitude when the divisor is zero. EX/MEM samples each masked term every clock. The response handshake qualifies the instruction metadata, saved RVFI operands and sign. MEM combines the terms and applies the sign.\n\nOne timing-driven change goes beyond the proposed divider wiring. MEM also selects the complete writeback value before registering it, shortening the WB forwarding path. The existing WB interlock and load replay protocol still qualify consumers.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested warning flags.\n- The single-retirement ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks. The arithmetic arrays are exercised by simulation.\n- All 113 cocotb cases passed, including 27 ALU cases. New cases cover divisor magnitudes 65,535, 65,536 and 65,537, signed operands, setup overflow, backpressure, class changes and dependent WB consumers.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference ISS with and without bus stalls. Stalled ten-iteration CoreMark passed all five CRCs and the UART validation check in 3,140,795 marker cycles.\n\n## Timing\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 76.231, 86.926 and 86.926 MHz. The median gives 276.76 CoreMark iterations/s, 4.16% above the champion frequency at the same cycle count. It falls 0.367 MHz short of the 87.293 MHz replacement threshold. The median placement's worst path runs between EX forwarding-control registers. The divider final trial no longer appears among its five worst setup paths.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
