{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_173","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --untracked-files=all; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Short-loop instruction replay\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,767,124 marker cycles**, 11.12% fewer than the champion's 4,238,604. The local Gowin median of **59.797 MHz** yields **158.73 iterations/s**, an 8.59% gain over 146.18.\n\n## Implementation\n\nIF allocates one 64-word interval on an accepted live conditional branch predicted taken with an aligned displacement of −252 through −4 bytes. A contained loop keeps the existing interval. Each ready miss inside the bounds records the instruction, its direct target and its branch and JAL class bits. A filled slot can issue through instruction-bus stalls using the current direction counter. Reset, an accepted overlapping store or an advancing FENCE.I invalidates the interval.\n\nEX also checks a predicted direct target against the word address actually in IF. This makes retirement-PC continuity locally checkable. Separate direct and JALR address adders let the direct-target comparison start independently of register forwarding. The PC register stores the word address. EX resolves misalignment from the full byte target.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled.\n- All 95 cocotb cases passed. Directed cases cover four unavailable fetches across two iterations, a stalled byte patch followed by FENCE.I, independent store and fence invalidation, a hole, an address alias, 64- and 65-word spans, and a nested loop with a load-use interlock.\n- All eight RVFI comparisons passed across selftest and three random programs with ready and stalled buses. Stalled CoreMark matched all five canonical CRCs and emitted the validation message.\n- The full single-retire ALTOPS suite passed all 53 checks, including register consistency, retirement-PC continuity, liveness and retirement-order uniqueness.\n\n## FPGA timing\n\n| Gowin placement | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 58.898 MHz |\n| 1 | 59.797 MHz |\n| 2 | 59.797 MHz |\n\nPlacement 0 used 3,470 logic cells, 775 registers and eight BSRAMs. All three placements cleared 57.601 MHz. Replacing the active interval clears its filled mask, so alternating between disjoint loops incurs refill latency.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_174","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the 64-word loop buffer with store and FENCE.I invalidation. EX verifies each predicted direct target against the fetch PC.\n\nTen CoreMark iterations took **3,767,124 marker cycles**, 11.12% fewer than baseline. Median Gowin Fmax was **59.797 MHz**, yielding **158.73 iterations/s**, an **8.59% improvement**.\n\nLint, all 53 formal checks, 95 cocotb cases, eight RVFI trace comparisons, and all CoreMark CRCs passed. [Implementation notes](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep6/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-002-r5s1/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md)"}}
