{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_110","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --ignored | head -n 24; git diff --check; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n!! .agent.last\n!! .agent.log\n!! cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n!! formal/riscv-formal\n# Decode-time operand selection and aligned LW bypass\n\nThe local ten-iteration CoreMark run took **3,344,675 marker cycles**. The median Gowin Fmax was **64.611 MHz**, giving **193.18 iterations/s**, 11.42% above the supplied champion.\n\nDecode compares each source with the effective EX, MEM and WB destinations. The youngest match selects the bus that will carry its value during execution. A zero source selects no bus. ID/EX captures the two source masks, the two ALU masks, raw register reads and the same-edge WB value together. AUIPC selects PC for ALU A, and immediate operations select the immediate for ALU B. Branches, JALR, store data, multiply and RVFI use the independently selected register operands.\n\nAn aligned LW can forward the returning memory word directly to its immediate consumer. The existing data-memory hold keeps that consumer in EX until the transfer completes. A misaligned LW yields to an older writer, and decode waits for WB on dependent subword loads and multiplies.\n\nThe existing forwarding module now performs the decode comparisons. The mask registers live inside the ID/EX payload so they share its advance enable. A shared address-adder variant also passed local checks. The final design uses the original address path, which achieved a higher median Fmax with 32 fewer registers.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All six cocotb suites passed, including 28 pipeline cases.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and three random programs against the ISS, both with and without memory stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed all five canonical CRC checks, emitted `Correct operation validated.` and reported no out-of-bounds access.\n- Directed tests checked the one-clock LW-to-MUL gap and two-clock MUL-to-ADD gap on live and replayed instructions. They also exercised a stalled load/load/store chain, a dependent branch, competing writers, trapping LW, x0, same-edge WB and independent ALU and RVFI operands.\n\n## Timing\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 65.594 MHz |\n| 1 | 64.611 MHz |\n| 2 | 64.611 MHz |\n\nThe limiting path in all three placements runs between registers in the existing divider. The measured median was 0.667 MHz below the scratch prototype. Placement 0 used 4,205 logic cells, 1,109 registers, eight block RAMs and six DSP blocks.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
