{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git status --short --untracked-files=all; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n M cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\n# Operand capture and forwarding at issue\n\nID/EX captures both asynchronous register-file reads and their four one-hot forwarding enables. The two 32×32 distributed RAM copies share a write port and a resettable written mask. Both ports bypass a concurrent WB write. While ID holds, a retiring write updates each matching captured operand.\n\nIssue gives the younger EX writer priority. A MEM bus hold freezes the selects. When MEM drains during replay or a divide, the saved match selects WB only if the producer passes its alignment check. Otherwise the consumer uses its captured value. Raw decode controls advance with the operands, and slot validity, replay and divide completion gate the three EX/MEM side effects. Retirement uses the target's configured single RVFI channel.\n\nThe EX writer qualifier checks jump alignment directly from the low operand bits. This keeps the branch comparator off the forwarding decision. The direct-jump check relies on word-aligned fetch PCs, and the JALR check includes the carry from bit zero before the target is rounded down to an even address.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with no warnings.\n- All 107 cocotb cases passed. The misaligned LW issued no bus read, and the replayed ADD retired with operands 17 and 17 and result 34. The register-file regression checked both RAM copies across reset and rewrite.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the ISS on zero-wait buses and with both stall generators enabled.\n- Ten CoreMark iterations passed all five CRC checks in 3,207,017 marker cycles, from cycle 11,974 to 3,218,991.\n- The fast ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks on the first run of the final design, including register consistency, liveness and unique retirement order.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\nPlacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 75.753, 77.806 and 77.806 MHz. The median yields **242.61 CoreMark iterations/s**, a 4.91% gain over 74.162 MHz at the same cycle count. Placement 0 used 4,356 logic cells, 1,255 registers and eight block RAMs. The median placement's critical path now runs from the predicted-load block RAM through the ALU to the EX/MEM result register.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
