{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_166","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\"'^place_option|score Fmax'\"' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/row-timing-final.log\"\ncat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\ngit diff --check'","aggregated_output":"place_option 0: Fmax 76.072 MHz, 14 logic levels; logic 7521, registers 1489, LUT-RAM 312, BSRAM 6, DSP 6\nplace_option 1: Fmax 83.236 MHz, 13 logic levels; logic 7521, registers 1489, LUT-RAM 312, BSRAM 6, DSP 6\nplace_option 2: Fmax 83.236 MHz, 13 logic levels; logic 7521, registers 1489, LUT-RAM 312, BSRAM 6, DSP 6\nscore Fmax (median): 83.236 MHz\n# Load-table row prediction\n\nID captures the word row from the low ten bits of the register-file base plus the I-immediate. It also captures the XOR of that row with the sampled base row. EX applies this mask to an EX/MEM or adjacent-load bypass. For a WB bypass, MEM registers the XOR of the consumer displacement and the producer's estimated word slice. The registered rs1 bypass enables select the table row.\n\nThe WB row holds through data-bus stalls. When a load consumer replays, MEM uses the I-immediate of the held consumer. MEM verifies the saved row and tag against the full effective address before using the predicted value. Table updates use the true address.\n\nThe forwarding unit also distinguishes WB load data from WB arithmetic data when it registers the operand enables. This lets EX compute the true address from the selected WB register directly. The first implementation used the existing architectural WB mux for that operand and measured 79.865 MHz median.\n\nDirected pipeline tests cover byte carries, a negative displacement, a stale forwarded pointer, a WB row held through a bus stall, a late multiply result, and the displacement used on replay. They check bus requests, retirement, dependent timing, and wrong-path stores.\n\nVerilator lint and all 114 cocotb cases passed. Local ALTOPS formal passed all 53 checks on the retained RTL. Co-simulation passed for selftest, three random programs, and CoreMark. The ten-iteration run with both stall generators enabled passed all five CRC checks in 3,134,538 bracketed cycles, saving 6,257 cycles against the champion.\n\nGowin measured 76.072, 83.236, and 83.236 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 83.236 MHz median yields 265.54 iterations/s and falls short of the 87.12 MHz replacement threshold. The longest path in the median placement runs from the divider quotient register to the EX/MEM ALU result. Three of the next four paths end at load-table occupancy bits.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
