{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"ls formal/riscv-formal && cat formal/checks_si.cfg && cat bench/programs/Makefile && cat Makefile | head -n 76 && rg -n 'syn_ramstyle|predict|timing|BRAM' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "COPYING\nCodeOfConduct\nREADME.md\nbus\nchecks\ncores\ndocs\ninsns\nmonitor\ntests\n[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\nCROSS   := riscv32-unknown-elf\nCC      := $(CROSS)-gcc\n\n# CoreMark build knobs. Defaults are the canonical EEMBC reporting config\n# (2K data size, -O3); tools/eval/fpga.py's COREMARK_EXPECTED CRCs assume\n# exactly these defaults.\n# - COREMARK_SIZE: TOTAL_DATA_SIZE in coremark.h (#ifndef-guarded). 2000 is\n#   the EEMBC canonical reporting config (used by VexRiscv et al.); 6000\n#   makes the matrix/list/state working sets ~3x larger so the score is\n#   harder to inflate via register-resident working sets.\n# - COREMARK_OPT: gc\n…[8244 of 11316 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:117829e0c1ade81a]…\nMark's divide-free timing window.\n28:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r10s2 (regression, +1.29%): Splitting 512 direction bits from 128 shared strength bits saved 13,992 timed CoreMark cycles, but training from `correction_q` left the path from the successor BRAM to `predicted_pc_q` limiting median Fmax to 79.029 MHz, below the 81.62 MHz needed for replacement.\n29:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r11s0 (regression, +2.89%): Capturing the eighth radix-16 digit on response acceptance let MEM read the divider state directly, shifting the critical path from the final EX trial to the load predictor’s `saved_raw` path to ID/EX `rs2_val` and limiting median Fmax to 85.863 MHz against an 87.293 MHz gate.\n34:- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r12s2 (regression, -1.52%): Rotating each load-predictor row to the last byte offset saved 218 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but forwarding `saved_word` through the next load’s index adder, table read and sign extension to `predicted_value` cut median Fmax from 83.455 to 82.178 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
