{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md; cat formal/checks_si.cfg'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.29%): Folding combinational DIV/REM into four steps of eight quotient bits shortened the EX critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 31.097 MHz, while timed cycles stayed flat because CoreMark executes all five DIVU sites outside its timing markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.82%): Predicting the next PC from decode operands and flushing only when EX resolved a different successor saved a fetch slot on correctly predicted taken transfers, cutting the prototype's timed CoreMark cycles by 7.3%.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +37.09%): Cutting the `remainder_q` feedback chain from eight to five trial subtractions per clock raised median Gowin Fmax to 43.681 MHz and moved the critical path to the ID/EX operand registers, while the three extra rounds per divide cost no timed cycles because CoreMark executes no DIV/REM between markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +43.23%): Registering 3D in a setup cycle let each radix-4 digit test D, 2D and 3D in parallel, raising Fmax by cutting the divider's remainder feedback path from eight dependent subtract/select stages to three.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.63%): Reusing taken targets in a 128-entry fetch-PC BTB saved 301,480 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, a 6.7% reduction too small to offset the radix-4 divider's clock advantage (45.64 vs 32.885 MHz).\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +8.66%): Registering the forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum at EX/MEM kept the multiplier DSP and EX result mux off the `dmem_addr` path, raising median Gowin Fmax from 45.640 to 49.590 MHz with the same timed CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (regression, +11.24%): A one-entry fall-through buffer let decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261\n…[3318 of 6390 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9a10b69438f923e7]…\nr    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
