{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-001-r4s0.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated the [micro_opt hypothesis](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep6/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261122-001-r4s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r4s0.yaml). It moves multiplication into MEM so operand forwarding and the DSP operate in separate cycles.\n\nThe prototype reached **143.72 CoreMark iterations/s**, 20.69% above the 119.08 champion. Median Gowin Fmax rose from 50.042 to 60.916 MHz, while bracketed cycles increased by 0.86%. All five CoreMark CRCs matched, and all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks passed.id: hyp-20261122-001-r4s0\ntitle: Register forwarded multiply operands at EX/MEM\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's worst reported path runs from the register file through\n  EX and the ALU to a data RAM address pin. The address comes from the\n  same result mux as the four multiply operations. In round 3, giving\n  memory its own registered address raised Fmax by 8.66% on the earlier\n  core. Moving the DSP across the EX/MEM boundary also separates the\n  operand forwarding muxes from the multiplication.\n\n  On the current champion, a scratch implementation reached 56.028,\n  60.916 and 60.916 MHz with Gowin's three placement options. Ten\n  iterations with both bus stalls took 4,238,604 bracketed cycles, up\n  36,332 cycles (0.86%). All five CoreMark CRCs matched. The median\n  clock gives 143.72 iterations/s, 20.69% above 119.08. The worst\n  path now ends at the fetch PC in all three placements.\nhypothesis: |\n  Execute MUL, MULH, MULHSU and MULHU in MEM. The existing EX/MEM\n  registers already capture the forwarded operands and opcode. Use those\n  registers as the DSP inputs and capture the selected product half in\n  MEM/WB. Independent multiplies can issue on consecutive clocks.\n\n  Treat a multiply destination as available at the same point as a load\n  destination. When its nonzero rd matches either raw source index of\n  the next instruction, use the existing load-use bubble and forward the\n  product from MEM/WB. Both indices matter \n…[1216 of 4288 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:aa12146baadd748b]…\nnd kill it with the existing decoded controls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Set ENABLE_MULTIPLY to zero on the EX instance. The existing rs1_val and write_data registers capture forwarded rs1 and rs2 on the EX/MEM advance edge.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Drive the multiplier with in.rs1_val, in.write_data and in.ctrl.alu_op. On MEM/WB advance, select its output for reg_q.alu_result when in.ctrl.is_mul is set. The ordinary integer writeback and forwarding paths then carry the product.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Feed mem_read OR is_mul to the existing load-use interlock, so both raw register indices receive the same forwarding protection for loads and multiplies.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check adjacent retirements for independent multiplies, one bubble for a dependent pair, all four product selections, writes to x0, and a raw rs2 match inside an immediate. Exercise product forwarding to a branch and store and across a stalled intervening store.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
