1.24%): A one-entry fall-through buffer let decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261\n…[4827 of 7899 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6f2360375e88b143]…\nt to 231.25 iterations/s at the same 3,207,017 timed cycles.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r9s0 (regression, +6.53%): Splitting ADD/ADDI from the other EX/MEM results left operand forwarding and decode qualification on the longest paths at 71.092 MHz median, while capturing register-file operands in ID and predecoding branch comparisons helped raise the median to 79.001 MHz at the same 3,207,017 timed CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r9s1 (regression, +4.91%): Capturing register-file operands and one-hot bypass selects together in ID/EX raised median Fmax from 74.162 to 77.806 MHz at the same 3,207,017 timed CoreMark cycles, leaving the path from the predicted-load BRAM through the ALU to the EX/MEM result register as the timing limit.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r9s2 (improvement, +7.91%): Caching one extended value per word let 65,415 of 83,971 adjacent CoreMark subword consumers skip the load-use bubble when the offset, width and signedness matched, with WB reusing the same verified value.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
