{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r12s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261123-001-r12s0\ntitle: Narrow the shared divider trials and register their residuals in groups of four\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion tests fifteen 36-bit divisor multiples per radix-16 digit.\n  Its median placement has a path from quotient_q through the final trial\n  to the EX/MEM result. Round 11 reached 86.926 MHz with separate arrays\n  for two divisor ranges. At 3,140,795 marker cycles the replacement gate\n  is just over 87.29 MHz. CoreMark executes no divides between its markers.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use one divider with four digit widths. For unsigned divisor magnitude\n  D below 2^11 consume four dividend bits per digit. At 2^11, 2^18 and\n  2^25 switch to three, two and one bit respectively. If the digit width\n  is s, setup takes the high 32-7s bits of the dividend as its prefix.\n  For s = 1, 2 or 3 this prefix is strictly below D and the initial\n  quotient digit is zero. For s = 4 the existing four-bit lookup gives\n  the initial digit and remainder. Prepare the odd multiples in parallel.\n  Process the seven remaining digits with six registered rounds and the\n  existing held response cycle.\n\n  Issue trial k only for k < 2^s. Trial 1 needs 32 data bits, trials\n  2-3 need 27, trials 4-7 need 21 and trials 8-15 need 15. These bounds\n  cover both kD and every eligible partial dividend. The largest class\n  consumes successive prefixes of a 32-bit dividend. Use a separate borrow\n  bit and mark ineligible trials as losing before selecting the largest\n  fitting digit. A digit wins when it fits and its successor does not,\n  with digit zero always fitting. Register the winning residual shifted\n  left by s with the next dividend group in its low bits, so each trial\n  starts from a registered partial dividend. The fifteen subtractors\n  total 290 data bits versus 540 for fifteen 36-bit trials.\n  Pack each s-bit dividend group into the low\n  bits of a nibble. Shift the stream by a fixed four bits, append each\n  quotient digit, and compact the eight nibbles according to the class.\n\n  At the EX/MEM \n…[1293 of 4365 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f0fa2a4f91a7807e]…\n to the load-table predicted-value register.\n  At the measured cycle count that placement delivers 285.66 iterations/s.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Implement the four radix classes, packed digit stream, tapered trials and grouped result registers. Expose their MEM result alongside the existing response.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Route the registered divide terms from the ALU to MEM and remove the final trial from the fast EX result. Capture the negate flag on transfer.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the completed divide magnitude in the EX/MEM bundle alongside the existing negate flag.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Select the dedicated divide magnitude for signed correction and WB on a valid DIV or REM.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: Exercise both sides of the 2^11, 2^18 and 2^25 thresholds, highest eligible digits, signed magnitudes, zero, overflow and held responses.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
