{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r15s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-001-r15s0\ntitle: \"Read load-table bytes in access order using mixed-width block RAM\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported placement-0 path runs from effective_addr[14] to\n  the load-table write port. A change of subword format currently verifies\n  the saved bytes, selects the resident word, formats it and rewrites the row.\n  Three round-14 attempts to buffer these writes lost median Fmax. A raw-word\n  table could eliminate format refreshes and give an adjacent consumer the\n  requested bytes on the first resident access, including after a word store.\nhypothesis: |\n  Implement the existing 256-row data payload with four synchronous byte\n  read ports. Each port sees the same accepted 32-bit word writes. For port\n  j, read byte address {lookup_index, lane XOR j} where lane is the exact\n  two-bit sum of the forwarded base and displacement low bits. Infer the\n  ports as replicated wide-write, narrow-read block RAM. Keep the current\n  split row index and register lane, width, signedness and tag alongside\n  the read. Qualify the result against the true cacheable address, alignment,\n  occupancy and same-row write collision in MEM. Hold all four samples and\n  controls together across a data-bus stall.\n\n  Port 0 always returns the addressed byte. Ports 0 and 1 supply an aligned\n  halfword, and ports 0 through 3 supply an aligned word in little-endian\n  order. Concatenate and extend these outputs once for both EX forwarding\n  and hit writeback. Invert the same XOR permutation on the same four\n  samples for the raw RVFI word. This construction makes the reported byte\n  lanes agree with the bypass even when formal explores arbitrary RAM data.\n  It removes the saved format, per-byte equality tests and format refresh\n  writes. Write the RAMs directly from an accepted cacheable read miss or\n  aligned full-word store. An accepted narrow store invalidates its row.\n  Preserve miss replay, MMIO transactions and misalignment traps.\n\n  The experiment should reduce marker cycles on resident format changes\n  and shorten the RAM write path. The timing risk is the block-RAM output\n  through sign extension and EX forwarding. Measure ten marker-bracketed\n  iterations and all three Gowin placements against 282.47 iterations/s.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute the exact two-bit byte lane in parallel with the two existing partial row adders.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect the producer's lane, width and signedness to the synchronous payload lookup.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement four XOR-addressed byte reads, share their formatted hit value with WB, reconstruct the raw RVFI word, and simplify row maintenance to fills and store updates.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Require adjacent retirement for all aligned resident formats, including a first narrow load after SW. Use distinct bytes with different sign bits to check every legal lane, canonical RVFI words, masks, miss replay, collisions, MMIO and held requests.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
