{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 8 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Shorten the divider feedback path with parallel radix-4 digit selection\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Gowin traces the champion's 37.434 ns worst path from the divider's remainder\\nregister back to itself. Each clock crosses eight dependent trial subtractions\\nand remainder selections. The prior CoreMark trace counted zero DIV or REM\\ninstructions between the markers. A shorter feedback path can therefore raise\\nFmax with negligible cost to the measured 4,491,485 cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Gowin traces the champion's 37.434 ns worst path from the divider's remainder\\nregister back to itself. Each clock crosses eight dependent trial subtractions\\nand remainder selections. The prior CoreMark trace counted zero DIV or REM\\ninstructions between the markers. A shorter feedback path can therefore raise\\nFmax with negligible cost to the measured 4,491,485 cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the binary restoring round with three radix-4 digit selections per\\nclock. For each digit, bring down two dividend bits and subtract D, 2D and 3D\\nin parallel. The borrow bits select the largest multiple that fits, its\\nresidual and the corresponding two quotient bits. Precompute the multiples\\nin a setup clock so their generation is outside the recurrence. Six identical\\nrounds consume a 36-bit dividend seeded with four leading zeros and yield\\nthe architectural quotient in the low 32 bits. This reduces the serial\\nsubtract/select depth from eight to three at the cost of two more parallel\\nsubtractors per digit and three additional clocks per division.\\n\\nAt the champion's marker cycle count, 41.4 MHz would yield about 92.2\\niterations/s, a 30% gain. The acceptance threshold is about 33.33 MHz at\\nthat cycle count. Check the post-route median and marker cycles separately.\\nRouting the parallel trials or the existing single-cycle multiply could\\nset the new clock limit.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 30, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"r\n…[51001 of 54073 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5bdacdddd00b65cc]…\nipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3454614, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 4057, \"ff\": 883, \"seeds\": [46.315, 47.168, 47.168], \"perturbations\": [[0, 46.315, 17], [1, 47.168, 16], [2, 47.168, 16]], \"lutram\": 16, \"bsram\": 8, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_1_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -16.591, \"arrival_ns\": 26.886, \"logic_cells\": 19, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 17, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T23:01:49.579025Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +14.66%): Looking up the last successor word by predecessor PC and validating its address against the full fetch PC hid `imemReady` stalls even at taken targets, cutting the prototype\\u2019s timed CoreMark cycles by 17.8%, though the 61.962 MHz MEM-stage multiplier still delivered higher throughput (146.18 vs 136.54 iterations/s).\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
