{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.29%): Folding combinational DIV/REM into four steps of eight quotient bits shortened the EX critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 31.097 MHz, while timed cycles stayed flat because CoreMark executes all five DIVU sites outside its timing markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.82%): Predicting the next PC from decode operands and flushing only when EX resolved a different successor saved a fetch slot on correctly predicted taken transfers, cutting the prototype's timed CoreMark cycles by 7.3%.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +37.09%): Cutting the `remainder_q` feedback chain from eight to five trial subtractions per clock raised median Gowin Fmax to 43.681 MHz and moved the critical path to the ID/EX operand registers, while the three extra rounds per divide cost no timed cycles because CoreMark executes no DIV/REM between markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +43.23%): Registering 3D in a setup cycle let each radix-4 digit test D, 2D and 3D in parallel, raising Fmax by cutting the divider's remainder feedback path from eight dependent subtract/select stages to three.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.63%): Reusing taken targets in a 128-entry fetch-PC BTB saved 301,480 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, a 6.7% reduction too small to offset the radix-4 divider's clock advantage (45.64 vs 32.885 MHz).\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +8.66%): Registering the forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum at EX/MEM kept the multiplier DSP and EX result mux off the `dmem_addr` path, raising median Gowin Fmax from 45.640 to 49.590 MHz with the same timed CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (regression, +11.24%): A one-entry fall-through buffer let decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (improvement, +17.19%): Computing B-type and JAL targets from the current instruction in IF/ID keeps targets exact even when the 128 two-bit direction counters alias, so correctly predicted taken transfers save a fetch slot and EX detects mispredictions with a one-bit XOR.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261122-001-r4s0 (regression, +19.48%): Feeding the MEM-stage multiplier from EX/MEM registers split operand forwarding and DSP multiplication across two cycles, raising median Fmax from 50.0 to 60.3 MHz while the load-use interlock for multiply results added just 0.86% to timed CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +14.66%): Looking up the last successor word by predecessor PC and validating its address against the full fetch PC hid `imemReady` stalls even at taken targets, cutting the prototype’s timed CoreMark cycles by 17.8%, though the 61.962 MHz MEM-stage multiplier still delivered higher throughput (146.18 vs 136.54 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.43%): Forwarding LW, LH, LHU and LBU from MEM to EX eliminated load-use bubbles and saved 202,756 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, while extending the bypass to signed LB in simulation saved only 1,536 more cycles and required selecting a sign bit from four byte lanes.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r5s1 (regression, +8.59%): Replaying filled words from a 64-word backward-branch window hid consecutive `imemReady` stalls, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 11.1% in the prototype versus 18.3% for successor replay, which also covered fetches outside short loops.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 (improvement, +18.61%): Computing branch and JAL targets when filling the successor table kept the target adder off the BRAM read path, letting the prototype select a stored target on 74,650 verified instruction issues while `imemReady` was low during timed CoreMark.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
