{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r6s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261123-001-r6s0\ntitle: Forward load results to EX with registered byte-lane selects\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,463,993 marker cycles for ten CoreMark iterations.\n  It can supply a successor during an instruction-bus stall, but even a\n  ready LW forces its dependent successor to wait for WB. On the preceding\n  core, bypassing four load types saved 202,756 cycles. The champion's\n  placement-zero critical path ends at data RAM after traversing the general\n  EX ALU, so the new bypass also needs a short path to the next load address.\nhypothesis: |\n  Let a dependent instruction enter EX immediately after any legal load.\n  Compute the load format controls in the producer's EX cycle from its\n  width, signedness and the low two bits of the sum of forwarded rs1 and\n  the immediate.\n  Register those controls and the full effective address at EX/MEM. In MEM,\n  select bytes and the extension sign in parallel from the raw bus word.\n  Give this formatted value its own input to each EX operand mux. This puts\n  lane decoding before the memory response and allows an accepting load and\n  its consumer to advance on the same edge. Consecutive pointer loads are\n  a timing stress case because a returned pointer feeds the next address.\n\n  A scratch prototype passed the canonical CoreMark UART and CRC checks in\n  3,260,731 bracketed cycles, saving 203,262 cycles (5.87%) with both\n  random stall generators active. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2\n  reached 56.085, 62.537 and 62.537 MHz. The median gives 191.79\n  iterations/s, 10.62% above the champion. At that cycle count,\n  59.14 MHz clears the acceptance margin.\n  Placement zero used 4,133 logic cells and 1,211 FFs. The prototype\n  passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks and 98 cocotb cases.\n  Its retirement traces matched the ISS on selftest and the three random\n  programs with zero-wait buses and with both stall generators enabled.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 11\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    descr\n…[1190 of 4262 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c5fdc7575419bd2d]…\ns, which are all zero for LW and unsigned loads.\n      Supply that result to both MEM/WB and EX. Export write permission after\n      misalignment checking so a trapping load cannot shadow an older writer.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the MEM load value and write permission to EX forwarding.\n      Reserve the existing WB-only use interlock for multiplies. Keep the\n      dmem hold on both the load and its consumer until the bus accepts.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      In predicted_load_and_multiply_consumers, require a one-cycle\n      retirement gap for LW to MUL and a two-cycle gap for MUL to ADD,\n      including when IF issues the cached successor. Check adjacent\n      ready-bus retirements for each load width, sign and legal lane. Cover\n      loads feeding another address, a branch and store data. Vary read data\n      during a multi-cycle hold. Check forwarding priority for a misaligned\n      load sharing rd with an older writer and for a load targeting x0.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
