{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor i,l in enumerate(open('.tmp/agent-logs/impl.hyp-20261123-001-r8s0.log'),1):\n try:\n  item=json.loads(l).get('item') or {}\n except ValueError:\n  continue\n if i==289 or (i>=180 and item.get('type')=='agent_message'):\n  print(i,item.get('text',item.get('aggregated_output',''))[-7500:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "180 Delaying the fetch-table write did not improve median timing, so I reverted it. The load table still delivers the expected cycle count. I’m checking the dependent operand path and keeping the raw-word path for writeback and RVFI.\n259 The added misaligned-halfword test passes. A trapping `LH` replays its consumer with the older register value. Formal has passed 52 of 53 checks on the initial implementation. The channel-0 register-consistency proof is still running.\n289   logic mem_op;\n  ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n\n  // The data and tag share a synchronous block RAM port. Occupancy is\n  // separate so reset can invalidate every row in one edge.\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"block_ram\" *) logic [41:0] words [0:255];\n  logic [255:0] occupied;\n  logic [7:0] candidate_index, update_index;\n  logic [9:0] candidate_tag;\n  logic candidate_valid, update, install;\n  logic [31:0] candidate_word, raw_word, fill_word;\n\n  assign update_index = in.effective_addr[9:2];\n  assign load_hit = in.ctrl.mem_read && !mem_misalign && candidate_valid &&\n                    {12'b0, candidate_tag, candidate_index} == in.effective_addr[31:2];\n  assign raw_word = load_hit ? candidate_word : dmem_rdata;\n\n  // Wide writes install every format at once. The producer addresses one\n  // narrow slot per RAM so the consumer sees an already extended operand.\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"block_ram\" *) logic [7:0]  low_bytes    [0:1023];\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"block_ram\" *) logic [7:0]  middle_bytes [0:2047];\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"block_ram\" *) logic [15:0] upper_halves [0:2047];\n  logic [2:0] middle_slot, upper_slot;\n  logic [7:0] low_q, middle_q;\n  logic [15:0] upper_q;\n  logic [63:0] middle_row;\n  logic [127:0] upper_row;\n  logic lookup_byte, lookup_narrow;\n\n  // Slots 2 and 3 both supply zero. This leaves the low address bits\n  // free to follow the lane even when a signed mask is unnecessary.\n  assign lookup_byte = lookup_width == 2'd0;\n  assign lookup_narrow = !lookup_width[1];\n  assign middle_slot[2] = lookup_byte && lookup_sext;\n  assign middle_slot[1] = lookup_byte \n…[3581 of 6653 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f094c09ee54034f4]…\nompleted in 3,125,942 marker cycles (11,679 to 3,137,621).\n\nThe slot selectors use the duplicate zero entries to simplify address generation. The lane comes from the low two bits of the forwarded effective-address sum. The fetch and register-file control paths retain their original timing.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 67.754, 69.330 and 69.330 MHz. The median gives 221.79 CoreMark iterations/s, 0.72% above the champion and below the 4.6% target. Placement 0 used 4,777 logic cells and 17 of 46 BSRAMs. The median critical path ran from register-file RAM through EX to the fetch successor table's occupancy enable.\n\nLocal formal is incomplete. The single-retirement ALTOPS gate passed 52 of 53 checks. The channel-0 register-consistency check (`reg_ch0`) is still solving at step 20 and has produced no counterexample.\nMon Nov 23 02:26:51 UTC 2026\nSBY  3:21:43 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:08  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\nSBY  3:26:43 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:10:08  waiting for solver (10 minutes)\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
