{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r11s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-002-r11s1\ntitle: Split division at the 16-bit divisor boundary\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  In the champion's 83.455 MHz median placement, a path from quotient_q[30]\n  through the final divider trial to EX/MEM's ALU result ties the worst\n  setup slack at -6.982 ns. Every digit currently tests fifteen 36-bit\n  multiples, and the resulting magnitude enters the ordinary EX forwarding\n  register even though a divide consumer interlocks until WB. RV32 arithmetic\n  bounds either the running remainder or the final quotient to 16 bits.\nhypothesis: |\n  Classify the unsigned divisor magnitude D at request acceptance. For\n  0 < D < 65536, the restored remainder is always below 65536. Appending\n  four bits gives a partial dividend below 2^20, and 15D also fits in\n  20 bits. Use a 20-bit radix-16 array with a separate borrow bit per\n  subtraction, eight quotient digits and a 16-bit remainder register.\n\n  For D >= 65536, the high half of the dividend is already below D and\n  the quotient fits in 16 bits. Start with that high half as the remainder\n  and consume the low half two bits per digit. The full-width radix-4\n  array needs three trial multiples. In setup resolve the first digit from\n  dividend[31:14], using an 18-bit partial and explicit overflow detection,\n  while registering 3D for later rounds. Both classes then take six\n  registered rounds and one final trial on the held response state.\n\n  During setup register mutually exclusive enables for class, quotient or\n  remainder, and zero. Capture each array's masked result in its own EX/MEM\n  data register. On a transferred divide, MEM combines the two terms and\n  applies the sign for WB. The fast EX result register now carries only\n  ordinary ALU and PC results. The two divide data registers can sample\n  every clock because only a valid divide in MEM consumes them and the\n  divider holds a pending response stable. Preserve signed overflow,\n  division by zero, ALTOPS and the existing backpressure protocol.\n\n  The split-array prototype passed 26 ALU tests, including cases on both\n  sides of 65536, and matched the ISS on four programs with both buses\n  stalled. It used 979 arithmetic cells versus 1,117 for the champion.\n  With the original EX result merge, the three placements reached 81.728,\n  83.525 and 83.525 MHz, giving 265.94 iterations/s at the median.\n  Both the original merge and separate result registers gave 3,140,795\n  CRC-validated marker cycles. The replacement threshold is 87.293 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Implement the two magnitude classes and masked response terms. Retain the combined ALU result for standalone tests and deliver ALTOPS through the same protocol.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Register the two divide terms independently at EX/MEM. Keep response handshaking, operand capture and replay qualification on the instruction metadata.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Route the registered divide terms to MEM alongside the EX/MEM bundle.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Combine the registered terms, apply the divide sign and select the signed result for a completing divide.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: Exercise magnitudes 65535, 65536 and 65537, both signs, and dividends one below, at and above multiples. Alternate the two classes on consecutive requests and hold the response before switching.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
