ue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (improvement, +17.19%): Computing B-type and JAL targets from the current instruction in IF/ID keeps targets exact even when the 128 two-bit direction counters alias, so correctly predicted taken transfers save a fetch slot and EX detects mispredictions with a one-bit XOR.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261122-001-r4s0 (regression, +19.48%): Feeding the MEM-stage multiplier from EX/MEM registers split operand forwarding and DSP multiplication across two cycles, raising median Fmax from 50.0 to 60.3 MHz while the load-use interlock for multiply results added just 0.86% to timed CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (regression, +14.66%): Looking up the last successor word by predecessor PC and validating its address against the full fetch PC hid `imemReady` stalls even at taken targets, cutting the prototype’s timed CoreMark cycles by 17.8%, though the 61.962 MHz MEM-stage multiplier still delivered higher throughput (146.18 vs 136.54 iterations/s).\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r5s0 (regression, +9.43%): Forwarding LW, LH, LHU and LBU from MEM to EX eliminated load-use bubbles and saved 202,756 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, while extending the bypass to signed LB in simulation saved only 1,536 more cycles and required selecting a sign bit from four byte lanes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
