decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261\n…[1193 of 4265 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5f733d6ec298d428]…\n signed LB in simulation saved only 1,536 more cycles and required selecting a sign bit from four byte lanes.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r5s1 (regression, +8.59%): Replaying filled words from a 64-word backward-branch window hid consecutive `imemReady` stalls, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 11.1% in the prototype versus 18.3% for successor replay, which also covered fetches outside short loops.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 (improvement, +18.61%): Computing branch and JAL targets when filling the successor table kept the target adder off the BRAM read path, letting the prototype select a stored target on 74,650 verified instruction issues while `imemReady` was low during timed CoreMark.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r6s0 (regression, +7.16%): Registering byte-lane and sign selects at EX/MEM let MEM select the result bytes and extension sign in parallel, so all five load types could feed a dependent EX instruction on the cycle memory responded, cutting the prototype’s timed CoreMark cycles by 5.87%.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
