{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r9s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261123-002-r9s1\ntitle: Register operand values and their bypass choices together at issue\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Both completed round-eight timing runs report a critical path from the\n  register file to the EX/MEM ALU-result register. In the champion's median placement the\n  register-file block RAM takes 2.260 ns from clock to data. The operand\n  still passes through forwarding and ALU input selection. Another near-critical\n  path starts at the registered source-register match, where EX still has to\n  resolve writer priority. At 3,207,017 cycles for ten iterations, 77.58 MHz\n  would clear the replacement threshold.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move operand collection to the ID/EX boundary. Read the incoming instruction's\n  two register indices from coherent 32-by-32 asynchronous distributed RAM\n  copies. A common resettable written mask supplies architectural zeros, and\n  write-through supplies a concurrent WB value on either port. Capture each\n  32-bit read in ID/EX fabric flip-flops. During a hold, update a captured\n  value only when a retiring nonzero destination matches that held source. This allows an older\n  writer to drain during replay and removes the stall signal from both RAM addresses.\n\n  Beside each captured operand, register all four forwarding enables. On issue,\n  compare the incoming rs with the nonzero destinations currently in EX and MEM.\n  Qualify each match with the writer that will actually advance, including\n  JALR target and load alignment traps. A matching EX producer takes priority\n  and selects the registered predicted load word or EX/MEM result according\n  to its type. A sole MEM match selects WB. Otherwise select the captured\n  operand. Freeze these enables during a MEM bus hold. When ID holds but MEM\n  drains, choose WB if the saved register match and MEM's post-trap write\n  enable are both set. Otherwise choose the captured operand. Clear both MEM\n  selections and the saved match on that edge. Retain the existing load-address\n  verification, local replay and WB-only interlo\n…[1858 of 4930 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ee737e8d92134acb]…\nd AND ctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Qualify the three EX/MEM side-effect enables when the slot advances. Feed the existing final predicted-word mux with the registered load_word select.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Export the post-alignment-trap register-write enable for the producer advancing to WB so a faulting load cannot mask an older captured value.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect WB destination and data to the held operands, and connect both advancing writer qualifiers and the EX load type to the two forwarding units.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Issue ADDI x5,x0,17 followed by a misaligned LW to x5 and ADD x7,x5,x5. Check that the load traps without a bus read and the replayed ADD retires both operands as 17 and its result as 34.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: Populate two nonzero registers, reset, and read both addresses on each port. Rewrite one address and verify both copies return the new word while the other address still reads zero.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
