{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261123-003-r10s2.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r10s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Train a split direction predictor from the registered fetch correction\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion mispredicted 54,467 of 529,588 advancing conditional branches\n  during the ten-iteration CoreMark bracket. Its 128 counters alias every 512\n  bytes of code. In a repeat of Gowin placement option 1, two of the five\n  longest paths ran from the forwarded rs1 match to the direction-counter\n  update registers. The fetch stage already registers the correction that\n  identifies whether the chosen successor was wrong.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use 512 direction bits indexed by PC[10:2] and 128 shared strength bits\n  indexed by PC[8:2]. Initialize each direction to fallthrough and each\n  strength to weak. Fetch uses the direction with the existing exact direct\n  target. Carry the sampled direction and strength with the instruction.\n  Four different direction entries share each strength bit, so an alias\n  can affect how readily a branch changes its prediction without overwriting\n  the other branches' learned directions.\n\n  Move training behind the EX edge. For an advancing legal conditional branch,\n  capture its index, sampled direction and strength, and the taken bit actually\n  selected in IF. In the following cycle recover the aligned outcome by XORing\n  that selected bit with the existing registered correction_q. This works even\n  when target-range or alignment checks forced a fallthrough despite a taken\n  table prediction. Set the new strength when the sampled table direction\n  matched the recovered outcome. A formerly strong entry keeps its direction,\n  and a formerly weak entry takes the recovered outcome. The existing registered\n  train enable suppresses writes for reset, replay, holds, jumps and FENCE.I.\n  Bypass the pending direction and strength writes to fetch independently using\n  their respective index widths. This removes counter-update logic from the\n  forwarded-operand path and preserves one update per advancing branch.\n\n  In scratch cosim the split table with ordinary EX outcome training finished\n  in 3,126,803 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all five CRCs correct,\n  saving 13,992 cycles. At that cycle count the 4.6 percent replacement margin\n  requires a median above 81.62 MHz. The clock gain depends on removing the\n  training path without lengthening the fetch-side direction bypass.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Implement the separate direction and strength tables, reconstruct the branch outcome from the registered correction, and forward each pending table write on its own index match.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Export the nine-bit training index and the branch's sampled predictor state on its existing advancing-branch enable.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Widen the training index and route the conditional branch's actual fetch decision from ID/EX to the training register.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise two branch PCs sharing only strength, full direction aliases, and a forced-fallthrough target. Verify retirement PCs, wrong-path store suppression and single training on release from a load replay.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
