{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -1 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Split division at the 16-bit divisor boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"In the champion's 83.455 MHz median placement, a path from quotient_q[30]\\nthrough the final divider trial to EX/MEM's ALU result ties the worst\\nsetup slack at -6.982 ns. Every digit currently tests fifteen 36-bit\\nmultiples, and the resulting magnitude enters the ordinary EX forwarding\\nregister even though a divide consumer interlocks until WB. RV32 arithmetic\\nbounds either the running remainder or the final quotient to 16 bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"In the champion's 83.455 MHz median placement, a path from quotient_q[30]\\nthrough the final divider trial to EX/MEM's ALU result ties the worst\\nsetup slack at -6.982 ns. Every digit currently tests fifteen 36-bit\\nmultiples, and the resulting magnitude enters the ordinary EX forwarding\\nregister even though a divide consumer interlocks until WB. RV32 arithmetic\\nbounds either the running remainder or the final quotient to 16 bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Classify the unsigned divisor magnitude D at request acceptance. For\\n0 < D < 65536, the restored remainder is always below 65536. Appending\\nfour bits gives a partial dividend below 2^20, and 15D also fits in\\n20 bits. Use a 20-bit radix-16 array with a separate borrow bit per\\nsubtraction, eight quotient digits and a 16-bit remainder register.\\n\\nFor D >= 65536, the high half of the dividend is already below D and\\nthe quotient fits in 16 bits. Start with that high half as the remainder\\nand consume the low half two bits per digit. The full-width radix-4\\narray needs three trial multiples. In setup resolve the first digit from\\ndividend[31:14], using an 18-bit partial and explicit overflow detection,\\nwhile