{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r11s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-003-r11s2\ntitle: Predict the load-table index from a forwarded base and an early carry estimate\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  In a repeat of the champion's 83.455 MHz Gowin placement, three of the\n  seven longest paths ran from WB read data or its result select to load-table\n  output registers. The table address currently waits for the architectural\n  WB mux, operand forwarding and two partial additions. MEM already verifies\n  the complete word address before the CPU can consume a predicted value.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the read row separately for each possible source of the load base.\n  For a register-file operand, compute (B + D)[9:2] in ID from the sampled\n  base B and I-immediate D using a ten-bit sum. Capture this row and the\n  mask B[9:2] XOR (B + D)[9:2] with the load. For a base forwarded from\n  EX/MEM or from an adjacent load, XOR its actual forwarded slice with the\n  saved mask. This predicts the carries in B + D using the earlier sample.\n  For a WB bypass, prepare the complete row at MEM/WB. XOR the younger\n  displacement bits [9:2] with the older load's predicted-value bits [9:2]\n  or the older non-load's EX/MEM ALU-result bits [9:2]. This source predicts\n  zero carries and can be wrong after an older load miss or late M result.\n  Select the four row candidates with the registered rs1 bypass enables.\n  This removes the WB result mux and address arithmetic from the path\n  between the WB register and the synchronous table read.\n\n  Hold the WB row with WB data and use the held consumer's displacement\n  when decode stalls. MEM must match the saved index and tag against the\n  full forwarded effective address and retain every occupancy, collision,\n  alignment and format check. Allocate and invalidate using the true\n  address. An incorrect estimate falls through to the existing bus and\n  dependent-replay path.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,134,538\n  bracketed cycles with both buses stalled, versus 3,140,795 for the\n  champion. It needs a median above 87.12 MHz to clear the replacement\n  margin. The fitness estimate depends primarily on that clock gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the decoded row and eight-bit carry-dependent XOR mask in ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the row from the low ten bits of the register-file base plus displacement, and its XOR mask, on ID advance.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Precompute the WB row using the consumer displacement and the producer estimate. Hold it with the WB data.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Drive lookup_index with the one-hot OR of the four source-specific row predictions.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire the eight-bit WB row to EX and select the incoming or held consumer displacement for MEM.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise a decode carry estimate invalidated by forwarding and a WB base estimate taken from a missed load. Update exact hit and miss expectations in load_index_discarded_carries for the new predictor. Check bus addresses, dependent replay, retirement and absence of wrong-path stores.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
