{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r15s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261123-003-r15s2\ntitle: Predict zero-offset returns from a registered four-entry return stack\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Every JALR currently costs a redirect, including a return immediately after\n  its caller supplied the link. Branch direction already has limited headroom.\n  The round-13 local-history predictor removed 45.5% of conditional misses but\n  saved 0.78% of marker cycles. A general 256-entry target-buffer prototype\n  saved 19,833 cycles on this champion, but its median fell to 76.088 MHz.\n  Calls provide return addresses without a PC-indexed target RAM or tag lookup.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a four-entry shift-register return-address stack for the standard x1\n  calling convention. On an advancing aligned JAL or JALR that writes x1,\n  push its architectural PC+4. Store bits [19:2] and a validity bit that\n  records whether the full link is in RAM. Pop on an advancing aligned\n  JALR x0,0(x1). Shift on every qualified push or pop, including an out-of-range\n  push, so nested calls retain their order. Overflow drops the oldest hint.\n  Reset and an advancing FENCE.I clear the four valid bits.\n\n  Recognize the exact return encoding on live instruction fetches and record\n  a return bit alongside each verified successor instruction at fill. When\n  the issued word is a return and the top entry is valid, predict that\n  address. Capture the top and a one-bit return selection in parallel with\n  the ordinary fetch PC calculation. Give registered EX correction priority.\n  Associate the exact issued hint with ID/EX and retain it through stalls.\n  Update the stack only once when the control transfer actually advances.\n\n  Check a predicted return against forwarded x1[31:1] in EX. For timing,\n  compare the captured, EX/MEM, WB and speculative-load operand candidates\n  to the saved return word in parallel and select their one-bit comparison\n  with the existing rs1 bypass enables. This keeps the 32-bit operand mux\n  and general JALR adder out of the return comparison. Bit zero is discarded\n  by JALR. A set bit one takes the existing misalignment trap and recovers to\n  PC+4. An aligned mismatch redirects to the actual forwarded x1 with bit zero\n  cleared, even though both prediction and resolution say taken. A load replay\n  or older memory stall suppresses correction and stack updates until release.\n\n  A scratch four-entry stack with comparison after forwarding took 3,128,081\n  cycles for ten iterations with both buses stalled and all five CRCs correct.\n  This saves 12,714 cycles versus the champion. At that count, acceptance\n  requires more than 92.42 MHz median. The timing hypothesis is that separating\n  the return PC register and moving comparison ahead of forwarding can reach\n  that clock. The cycle saving alone is insufficient.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Add the four-entry return stack, successor return flag and registered return PC selection. Use the selected instruction's hint and preserve redirect, hold and invalidation priority.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the issued return prediction bit and 18-bit target word in IF/ID and ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture and hold return metadata with its instruction through stalls and replay.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Validate the return against the selected forwarding source, recover a wrong target or misalignment, and emit one qualified push or pop per advancing transfer.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire the resolved stack action and architectural link address between EX and IF.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check nested calls, overflow, underflow, a re