cle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (regression, +11.24%): A one-entry fall-through buffer let decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.\n…[3590 of 6662 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:68002cf3e1290cd4]…\netch successor table’s occupancy bits.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r7s2 (regression, +4.67%): Forwarding preformatted byte and halfword loads with a registered one-hot bank select saved 81,075 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but the path from the fetch successor BRAM to its occupancy bits limited median Fmax to 67.376 MHz versus the round winner’s 70.620 MHz.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Forwarding from three asymmetric RAMs in EX while WB extends a separate raw word requires both paths to agree on every verified subword hit, and the `reg_ch0` register-consistency check was still at step 20 when the 45-minute formal budget expired.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r8s1 (regression, +3.09%): Reading the successor BRAM every clock, retaining its candidate across stalls and registering fills preserved CoreMark's 3,207,017 timed cycles, but the new critical path from the register file to the EX/MEM ALU-result register capped median Fmax at 72.800 MHz, below the 73.87 MHz acceptance gate.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
