{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'ls cores/bench/experiments && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r8s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"hypotheses\nlog.jsonl\nprogress.png\nrun_summary.json\nid: hyp-20261123-003-r8s2\ntitle: \"Pipeline acceptance and validity in the fetch successor predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion runs ten timed CoreMark iterations in 3,207,017 cycles at\n  70.620 MHz. Its reported 19.918 ns path ends at the fetch successor RAM.\n  The other two round-seven designs report paths ending at the same\n  predictor's occupancy bits. Today a late EX redirect or replay influences\n  both RAM enables and the 512-row occupancy update through fetch acceptance.\n  The predictor already hides instruction-bus stalls. Its port timing is\n  the opportunity to raise throughput at the existing hit rate.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read the 512-row successor predictor at the current PC index every clock.\n  Register slot availability and absence of an EX correction separately.\n  Their conjunction certifies the preceding lookup. Capture the raw\n  predecessor index every clock beside the RAM read. After an accepted lookup use\n  its synchronous RAM output directly. On the first subsequent hold or\n  redirect retain that output in a shadow register, and use the retained\n  candidate through further holds. Retain its predecessor index under the\n  same registered enable so the corrected fetch trains the right row.\n  Keep candidate validity tied to the logical lookup and compare its\n  full successor address with the actual PC\n  before issuing when imemReady is low.\n\n  Put a one-entry pipelined write port on predictor training. Capture the\n  preceding index, live-fill eligibility and full 70-bit successor record.\n  Combine registered eligibility with registered acceptance for the write.\n  Represent occupancy as 32 resettable group-valid bits and 32 masks of\n  16 rows in distributed RAM. The first fill of an invalid group writes\n  a one-hot mask. Later fills OR the row bit into its current mask. If a\n  lookup reads a group being updated, forward the new mask and group-valid\n  bit to the read snapshot. Sustain one fill per clock. Suppress a\n  candidate on a physical same-row read/write collision and on a same-row\n  fill still in flight. Reset and advancing FENCE.I invalidate occupancy,\n  pending training and candidate validity together. An EX correction still\n  wins PC selection immediately. Verified cached instructions, live fetch\n  priority and the direction predictor retain their current semantics.\n\n  A control prototype with the grouped directory and delayed RAM ports\n  completed the bracket in 3,207,017 cycles with all five CRCs correct.\n  Gowin measured 68.328, 71.963 and 71.963 MHz, yielding 224.39 iterations/s.\n  Its longest median-placement path still ended at the predecessor index\n  enabled by same-cycle acceptance. Capturing the index unconditionally\n  and qualifying it in the following cycle targets that remaining path.\n  The split-acceptance prototype reproduced the 3,207,017-cycle bracket\n  and all five CRCs, passed all 30 pipeline tests and matched the ISS on\n  selftest and all three random programs with both bus stalls enabled.\n  At this cycle count the acceptance threshold is 73.867 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement the free-running lookup and predecessor index capture, registered acceptance and training, and grouped row-valid directory with same-group mask forwarding.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Warm a successor, hold it across multiple stalled or redirected cycles with poisoned imem data, then check retirement. Exercise adjacent same-row fills and invalidate a pending fill with FENCE.I before fetching modified code.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
