{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'tail -6 \"$TMPDIR/mulraw/formal-summary\" && tail -8 \"$TMPDIR/mulraw/directed-summary\" && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r4s0.yaml && date -u'","aggregated_output":"SBY 23:49:01 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:58 (58)\nSBY 23:49:01 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\nSBY 23:49:01 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\nSBY 23:49:01 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\n\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n.                                                                        [100%]\n1 passed in 0.61s\nid: hyp-20261122-001-r4s0\ntitle: Move the multiply DSP between the EX/MEM and MEM/WB registers\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported worst path starts at the register file, crosses\n  EX and the ALU, and ends at a data RAM address pin. All four multiplies\n  feed the same EX result mux that drives this pin. On the previous core,\n  isolating the address from that mux raised median Fmax by 8.66%.\n  Registering the DSP inputs also cuts operand forwarding out of the\n  multiply path. The cost in cycles depends on how often the instruction\n  immediately after a multiply consumes the result.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give MUL, MULH, MULHSU and MULHU the same result-availability boundary\n  as a load. In EX capture the two forwarded operands and the decoded\n  multiply operation in EX/MEM. Compute the product in MEM and capture the\n  selected half as the ordinary integer writeback value in MEM/WB. This\n  cuts the series of operand forwarding, multiplication and EX result\n  selection that Gowin currently places on the register-file-to-RAM path.\n  Independent instructions can still issue every cycle, including another\n  multiply. An immediate dependent takes the existing load-use bubble and\n  consumes the result through MEM/WB forwarding. Compare both raw source\n  fields against a valid producer's nonzero rd, as the trace checker\n  observes both source values on every retirement. Keep the registered operands and operation together through a dmem\n  hold. Preserve all four ALTOPS functions at the new result boundary.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Define a shared combinational multiplier in this source file. Include its four exact ALTOPS formulas. Keep the ALU unit-test interface by enabling multiply by default and disabling it with a parameter in EX.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add an is_mul bit to the control bundle for the result interlock and MEM/WB selection.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Set is_mul for a validated OP instruction with funct7 equal to one and funct3 below four. Capture and kill it with the existing decoded controls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Select the fast ALU configuration. Use the existing EX/MEM rs1_val and write_data fields for the two forwarded inputs, with the operation in ctrl.alu_op.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Compute multiply from the EX/MEM operand registers. Select its result on the MEM/WB alu_result input for multiply instructions and retain that register on a memory hold.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Feed mem_read OR is_mul to the existing load-use interlock, so both raw register indices receive the same forwarding protection for loads and multiplies.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise immediate multiply consumers on both ports, independent and dependent back-to-back multiplies, x0, and signed high products forwarded into a branch or store across a dmem stall.\nSun Nov 22 22:49:03 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
