{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r3s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-002-r3s1\ntitle: Execute multiply in MEM using registered forwarded operands\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's reported 27.311 ns path starts at the register file, crosses\n  EX and the ALU, and ends at data memory. EX currently puts a full product\n  behind the forwarding muxes and feeds its selected result into the same\n  register used for data addresses. The earlier trace counted 93,960 MULs\n  in 4,491,485 marker cycles. Even one extra cycle per MUL costs about 2.1%\n  of the cycle budget, making a later result worth testing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move all four multiply operations to a pipelined MEM-stage multiplier.\n  EX captures the forwarded operands on the normal EX/MEM transfer. MEM\n  computes one signed 33-by-33 product with independently sign- or\n  zero-extended operands and delivers the selected half through MEM/WB.\n  Independent instructions, including successive multiplies, can issue\n  every cycle. Interlock an immediately dependent instruction for one cycle,\n  then forward the registered product from WB. This removes multiplication\n  from the combinational path between operand forwarding and the memory\n  address register.\n\n  Budgeting one extra cycle for every timed MUL gives 4,585,445 cycles\n  before stall overlap and bus-sequence effects. At that count the median\n  clock must exceed 48.74 MHz to clear the acceptance margin. A 52 MHz\n  median would yield about 113.4 iterations/s. The main risk is that the\n  branch redirect or divider recurrence sets the clock before that point.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Separate multiply from the EX result mux. Give the shared multiplier\n      registered EX/MEM operands and operation selection. Extend A as signed\n      for MULH and MULHSU and B as signed only for MULH. Select bits 31:0 for\n      MUL and 63:32 for high products. Preserve each multiply's ALTOPS formula\n      at the same new pipeline latency and retain the existing DIV/REM timing.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture post-forward rs1, rs2 and multiply mode together when EX/MEM\n      advances. Use the existing EX/MEM hold and kill controls so a stalled\n      older memory operation preserves the in-flight instruction.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compute the product from the EX/MEM payload and latch the selected\n      half into the MEM/WB arithmetic result slot on the usual transfer.\n      This puts it on both the existing WB bypass and architectural write\n      port. An invalid slot has no register or memory side effects.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Identify a valid MUL, MULH, MULHU or MULHSU in ID/EX and connect its\n      late-result indication to the same interlock used by loads.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Interlock a younger consumer of a pending nonzero multiply rd when it\n      actually reads that register. Include branch operands, store data and\n      the JALR base. Keep the older dmem hold ahead of bubble injection so a\n      multiply can advance exactly once and a consumer sees its WB result.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise the relocated multiplier with all existing exact-result\n      vectors, including mixed-sign high products. Retain divider coverage.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check independent back-to-back multiplies and MUL-to-ALU, MUL-to-store\n      and load-to-MUL dependencies across imem and dmem stalls. Check the\n      forwarded operands, channel-0 writeback and exactly one accepted store.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
