{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\ntitle: Execute multiplies in MEM from registered operands\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion retires ten CoreMark iterations in 4,202,272 cycles at\n  50.042 MHz. Its reported worst path crosses the register file and EX ALU\n  on the way to the data RAM address. The four multiply operations share\n  the EX result mux with load and store addresses. A prior trace counted\n  93,960 MULs among 2,922,015 instructions between the timing markers.\n  This makes a one-cycle penalty on immediate consumers affordable if a\n  register boundary cuts the multiplier out of address generation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move multiply execution across the existing EX/MEM register. EX captures\n  the two forwarded operands. During MEM a dedicated multiplier computes\n  the selected low or high word, and the MEM/WB register captures it as the\n  instruction's writeback value. Independent multiplies can issue on\n  consecutive clocks. A multiply with a nonzero destination uses the same\n  one-cycle dependency interlock as a load. A dependent instruction then\n  consumes the registered result through the existing WB bypass.\n\n  A scratch prototype on the champion passed the CoreMark UART and five\n  CRC checks with both buses stalled. Ten iterations took 4,238,604 marker\n  cycles, up 0.86%. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 55.659, 58.489\n  and 58.489 MHz. The median gives 137.99 iterations/s, a 15.88% gain\n  over 119.08. At that cycle count the acceptance threshold is 52.796 MHz.\n  Placement 0 used 3,185 LUT4s, 644 registers and six DSPs. All eight RVFI\n  trace comparisons passed across selftest and three random programs with\n  and without bus stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 16\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Extract the multiply arithmetic and its four RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n      cases from the combinational EX result mux. A multiply opcode can\n      take the existing zero default here.\n  - file: rtl/multiplier.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the extracted combinational module with op, a, b and out.\n      Use the existing unsigned product for MUL and MULHU, signed product\n      for MULH and signed-by-unsigned product for MULHSU. Carry over the\n      exact ALTOPS formula for each operation.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the multiplier from in.rs1_val and in.write_data, the forwarded\n      rs1 and rs2 already registered at EX/MEM, and from in.ctrl.alu_op.\n      On a multiply select the product at the D input of MEM/WB.alu_result.\n      Use the existing hold_wb behavior to retain WB forwarding data.\n      The memory interface continues to use the EX/MEM address.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Identify a valid ID/EX MUL, MULH, MULHU or MULHSU as a late writer\n      alongside loads. Pass that producer flag to the load-use interlock.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Generalize the load producer input to cover a late result. On a\n      source match with a nonzero destination, let the producer advance,\n      hold fetch and bubble ID/EX. Honor older dmem holds and divide waits.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Route the four existing multiply tests to the extracted multiplier\n      suite. Run the existing base ALU and divider tests on alu.\n  - file: test/test_multiplier.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise the extracted unit with every existing exact multiplication\n      vector and compare random 32-bit pairs against full-precision low,\n      signed high, mixed-sign high and unsigned high product references.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Include multiplier.sv in the core build. Check consecutive retirement\n      cycles for independent multiplies and immediate consumers that use\n      WB forwarding, including a branch, a store and another multiply.\n      Hold a multiply behind an older stalled memory access, then check\n      the operands, accepted stores and successive channel-0 orders.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
