{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict the next fetch PC from decode-stage register values\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline fetches PC+4 after every instruction. IF and ID share a cycle,\n  so each aligned taken branch or jump squashes one fetch slot when EX redirects.\n  Both source registers are already read in that cycle, including same-cycle WB\n  bypass. When these values are current, they give the exact branch direction\n  or indirect jump target. Restricting prediction to those signals keeps the\n  predictor off the combinational EX ALU result path.\n\n  A scratch RTL prototype ran the ten-iteration CoreMark timing bracket in\n  4,163,829 cycles with both stall generators active, down from 4,491,485.\n  All five CRCs matched. Gowin reported 5.128, 5.447 and 5.447 MHz across its\n  three placement options. The median gives 13.08 iterations/s, 7.9% above\n  the 12.12 baseline.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the successor of each accepted instruction in the combined IF/ID\n  stage. For a legal conditional branch, evaluate its funct3 comparison on\n  the two current register-file read values. Predict the PC-relative target\n  on true and PC+4 on false. For JAL use the J target, and for legal JALR\n  use (rs1_read + I-immediate) with bit zero cleared. Select the target only\n  if it is word-aligned and below 0x00100000. Otherwise predict PC+4.\n  A pending register write can make a prediction stale, so EX resolves every\n  transfer using its forwarded operands, alignment checks and trap rules.\n\n  When a valid control transfer advances through EX, compare its resolved\n  successor against the current registered fetch PC. That PC is the prediction\n  selected at the edge that admitted this instruction to ID/EX. A mismatch\n  redirects to the resolved successor and kills the younger instruction.\n  A match lets ID accept the current fetch without a control bubble. Hold\n  both the PC and ID/EX during a data-memory freeze. On release, correction\n  takes priority even when imemReady is low. A load-use bubble or unready\n  instruction bus must never consume a prediction. EX supplies the resolved\n  pc_next to RVFI and PC+4 as the jump link value.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Use the existing legal transfer decode, immediate, PC and WB-bypassed register reads. Compare rs1 and rs2 for conditional branches. Use one target adder with PC or rs1 as its base, then select a legal target or fall-through.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Load the predicted successor when the current instruction is accepted. Hold the registered PC on stalls and apply an advancing EX correction with priority over imem backpressure.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Compare the architectural successor of a valid advancing branch or jump with the registered fetch PC. Correct to fall-through on a taken prediction that resolves false or traps, and correct wrong JALR targets even when both paths predict taken.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire ID prediction to IF, the registered fetch PC to EX, and the EX mismatch to the existing redirect and squash controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check consecutive retirement cycles for a correctly predicted taken branch and its target with both buses ready. Exercise stale operands in both branch directions, a stale JALR target, misalignment and overlapping bus stalls. Check retirement PCs, link writes and suppression of a wrong-path store.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
