{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -i 'predictor|predict the next' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.29%): Folding combinational DIV/REM into four steps of eight quotient bits shortened the EX critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 31.097 MHz, while timed cycles stayed flat because CoreMark executes all five DIVU sites outside its timing markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.82%): Predicting the next PC from decode operands and flushing only when EX resolved a different successor saved a fetch slot on correctly predicted taken transfers, cutting the prototype's timed CoreMark cycles by 7.3%.\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict the next fetch PC from decode-stage register values\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches PC+4 after every instruction. IF and ID share a cycle,\\nso each aligned taken branch or jump squashes one fetch slot when EX redirects.\\nBoth source registers are already read in that cycle, including same-cycle WB\\nbypass. When these values are current, they give the exact branch direction\\nor indirect jump target. Restricting prediction to those signals keeps the\\npredictor off the combinational EX ALU result path.\\n\\nA scratch RTL prototype ran the ten-iteration CoreMark timing bracket in\\n4,163,829 cycles with both stall generators active, down from 4,491,485.\\nAll five CRCs matched. Gowin reported 5.128, 5.447 and 5.447 MHz across its\\nthree placement options. The median gives 13.08 iterations/s, 7.9% above\\nthe 12.12 baseline, at a cost of 471 additional LUT4s. The prototype passed\\nall 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches PC+4 after every instruction. IF and ID share a cycle,\\nso each aligned take