{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r4s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict the next fetch index for a one-cycle instruction RAM\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,202,272 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its\n  direction predictor already supplies the exact target of a correctly\n  predicted direct transfer, but the first instruction there still waits\n  if imemReady is low. This also happens on sequential fetches in hot\n  loops. Reading a synchronous instruction RAM one cycle ahead could\n  recover those slots without putting a full next-PC calculation ahead\n  of the RAM address register.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the row containing the next instruction as the current instruction\n  issues. Default to the low word index of PC+4. A 128-entry table indexed\n  by PC[8:2] records a partial source tag and the eight low target word\n  address bits of an aligned taken transfer. On a live tag match, read\n  that row instead. Train once per advancing legal control transfer in EX,\n  installing the taken row or clearing a matching entry on fall-through.\n  The predictor drives only an internal RAM read address.\n\n  Keep 256 fetched instruction words in a direct-mapped synchronous RAM.\n  Fill from accepted imem responses to aligned PCs in the 1 MiB RAM window.\n  Pipeline the issued read index with the RAM output. At delivery, require\n  a valid entry, a matching returned row and upper tag, and an in-range PC.\n  On a hit, decode the saved word even with imemReady low. On a miss, use\n  a ready bus word immediately or retry the actual PC. Suppress hits on\n  undefined same-row read/write collisions. Reset and an advancing FENCE.I\n  invalidate all entries. FENCE.I also refetches its successor after older\n  stores complete, taking priority over any younger fill.\n\n  The existing direct-branch predictor chooses the actual fetch PC from the\n  delivered instruction. Its one-bit EX mismatch check, precise trap rules\n  and single retirement channel still govern execution. A bad index guess\n  costs a RAM hit. EX recovery overrides a fetch hold. During a hold, keep\n  the valid word for that PC or re-read its actual row. The external imem\n  address always names the actual fetch PC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 9\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Implement next-index prediction, a synchronous tagged word RAM, hit qualification, refill and fence invalidation. Feed the delivered word to both decode and direct-target prediction.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Export qualified resolved transfer outcomes, source PC bits and target row. Serialize FENCE.I with a redirect to its architectural successor.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire the predictor training event and fence invalidation to IF, and use the combined fetch-valid signal for the front-end readiness interlock.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Warm two loops sharing a predictor index. Deny imem at a predicted target and check single-cycle delivery. Force a wrong row, a data hold and a stalled correction, then modify code across FENCE.I and check retired words, PCs, order and wrong-path stores.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
