decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261\n…[6773 of 9845 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:12af81c3bf24e1e8]…\nelow the 81.62 MHz needed for replacement.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r11s0 (regression, +2.89%): Capturing the eighth radix-16 digit on response acceptance let MEM read the divider state directly, shifting the critical path from the final EX trial to the load predictor’s `saved_raw` path to ID/EX `rs2_val` and limiting median Fmax to 85.863 MHz against an 87.293 MHz gate.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r11s1 (regression, +4.16%): Splitting at divisor magnitude 65,536 saved 138 arithmetic cells, and moving the radix-16 and radix-4 result merge from EX to MEM kept the final trials off the EX forwarding path, raising median Fmax from 83.525 to 86.9 MHz, short of the 87.293 MHz replacement threshold.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r11s2 (regression, -0.06%): Precomputing the WB load-table row and estimating forwarded-base carries in ID saved 6,257 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but the divider path from `quotient_q` to the EX/MEM ALU result capped median Fmax at 83.236 MHz and erased the throughput gain.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
