{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['round_id'] in (10,11) and d['id'].endswith(('r10s1','r11s0','r11s1','r11s2')):\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Split DIV/REM completion across EX and MEM. At request acceptance capture\noperand magnitudes, signs and operation. In setup, divide the top four\ndividend bits by the divisor using a four-bit lookup for divisors 1 to 15.\nA larger divisor leaves the prefix as remainder. For zero, emit digit 15\nand retain the prefix. In parallel register the odd divisor multiples up\nto 15D. Shifts supply all the even multiples.\n\nExecute six registered radix-16 rounds. Each round appends four dividend\nbits to the remainder and subtracts all fifteen nonzero multiples in\nparallel, using 36-bit multiples and a separate borrow bit. Select the\nlargest fitting multiple and its residual. During the response cycle,\nreuse the same trials on the stable state for the eighth and final digit.\nLatch the selected quotient or remainder magnitude and its negate flag on\ntransfer. MEM restores the sign and writes the result to MEM/WB. Interlock\nadjacent divide consumers like multiply consumers so they read the final\nsigned result. Preserve response backpressure, reset cancellation, saved\nRVFI operands and the ALTOPS result with the same handshake.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten CRC-validated CoreMark iterations in\n3,140,795 marker cycles at a median 79.413 MHz. That is 252.84 iterations/s,\na 1.3% gain. The 4.6% replacement threshold at this cycle count requires\nmore than 81.98 MHz.\n\nOn a DIV/REM response handshake, clock the eighth digit through the same\nquotient shift and remainder feedback registers that capture digits two\nthrough seven. Capture all 32 remainder bits on this edge. The next trial\nstill takes only remainder_q[27:0] and the next dividend nibble as inputs.\nDuring the following MEM cycle, select the completed quotient or remainder\ndirectly from those registers and apply the existing sign correction.\nThe fast EX result then bypasses the final trial and its transfer enable.\nThe feedback registers supply the divider result to MEM.\n\nA divide in MEM always advances at the next edge. A following divide\ncan overwrite the state on the same ed\n…[3099 of 6171 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0500a8fe199eb69f]…\nad's predicted-value bits [9:2]\nor the older non-load's EX/MEM ALU-result bits [9:2]. This source predicts\nzero carries and can be wrong after an older load miss or late M result.\nSelect the four row candidates with the registered rs1 bypass enables.\nThis removes the WB result mux and address arithmetic from the path\nbetween the WB register and the synchronous table read.\n\nHold the WB row with WB data and use the held consumer's displacement\nwhen decode stalls. MEM must match the saved index and tag against the\nfull forwarded effective address and retain every occupancy, collision,\nalignment and format check. Allocate and invalidate using the true\naddress. An incorrect estimate falls through to the existing bus and\ndependent-replay path.\n\nA scratch implementation ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,134,538\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalled, versus 3,140,795 for the\nchampion. It needs a median above 87.12 MHz to clear the replacement\nmargin. The fitness estimate depends primarily on that clock gain.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
