{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'micro_opt' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-410\"", "aggregated_output": "1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-c4b728b\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Fold DIV and REM into four eight-bit quotient steps\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"At 5.442 MHz the baseline scores 12.12 iterations/s. Its reported 199.837 ns\\npath crosses operand forwarding and the EX ALU. The full-width combinational\\ndivide and remainder operators are the likely source of the 156-cell path.\\nDisassembly of the 2K RV32IM -O3 CoreMar\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Shorten the divider feedback path to five quotient bits per clock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst reported Gowin path starts and ends at remainder_q[1].\\nIt crosses eight dependent trial subtractions in one clock and has a\\nreported arrival time of 37.434 ns. The prior CoreMark trace retired zero\\nDIV or REM instructions inside the t\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Give loads and stores a dedicated registered effective address\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst path on Gowin placement option 0 runs from the\\nregister file through the multiplier DSP and EX result mux to a data\\nRAM address pin, with a reported arrival time of 27.311 ns. This path\\nexists because dmem_addr takes the general EX/MEM a\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Register forwarded multiply operands at EX/MEM\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's worst reported path runs from the register file through\\nEX and the ALU to a data RAM address pin. The address comes from the\\nsame result mux as the four multiply operations. In round 3, giving\\nmemory its own registered address raised Fmax by 8.66% on t\n…[2271 of 5343 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:faef6854f99c5e3f]…\na 256-row data\\nlookup and word-versus-subword selection between a forwarded base and the\\nnext predicted-value register. Its 256-bit occupied vector also needs a\\nfull indexed read in that cycle. Ten \n32:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Write the final radix-16 digit back into the divider state\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Repeating the champion's 83.455 MHz Gowin placement exposed a path from\\nquotient_q[30] through a trial subtraction to EX/MEM alu_result[12].\\nThe divider already has registers for the quotient and remainder, and the\\ncompleted divide spends a full cycle in MEM b\n35:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Narrow the shared divider trials and register their residuals in groups of four\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion tests fifteen 36-bit divisor multiples per radix-16 digit.\\nIts median placement has a path from quotient_q through the final trial\\nto the EX/MEM result. Round 11 reached 86.926 MHz with separate arrays\\nfor two divisor ranges. \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +471.29%): Folding combinational DIV/REM into four steps of eight quotient bits shortened the EX critical path and raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 31.097 MHz, while timed cycles stayed flat because CoreMark executes all five DIVU sites outside its timing markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.82%): Predicting the next PC from decode operands and flushing only when EX resolved a different successor saved a fetch slot on correctly predicted taken transfers, cutting the prototype's timed CoreMark cycles by 7.3%.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +37.09%): Cutting the `remainder_q` feedback chain from eight to five trial subtractions per clock raised median Gowin Fmax to 43.681 MHz and moved the critical path to the ID/EX operand registers, while the three extra rounds per divide cost no timed cycles because CoreMark executes no DIV/REM between markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +43.23%): Registering 3D in a setup cycle let each radix-4 digit test D, 2D and 3D in parallel, raising Fmax by cutting the divider's remainder feedback path from eight dependent subtract/select stages to three.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +10.63%): Reusing taken targets in a 128-entry fetch-PC BTB saved 301,480 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, a 6.7% reduction too small to offset the radix-4 divider's clock advantage (45.64 vs 32.885 MHz).\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +8.66%): Registering the forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum at EX/MEM kept the multiplier DSP and EX result mux off the `dmem_addr` path, raising median Gowin Fmax from 45.640 to 49.590 MHz with the same timed CoreMark cycle count.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (regression, +11.24%): A one-entry fall-through buffer let decode issue a word saved during a load-use or data-memory stall even with `imem_ready` low, cutting timed CoreMark cycles by 3.1% versus 6.4% for direct-branch prediction at a similar Fmax.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261\n…[9132 of 12204 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:68efec8f7da56baf]…\nnLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
