and its transfer enable.\nThe feedback registers supply the divider result to MEM.\n\nA divide in MEM always advances at the next edge. A following divide\ncan overwrite the state on the same edge that MEM/WB samples the older\nvalue. Hold the seventh-digit state while a response is blocked\nand perform the final feedback update exactly once on acceptance. Keep\nthe ALU's pre-handshake result available to its existing callers. In\nALTOPS mode expose the request-latched substitute result to MEM fo\n…[8642 of 11714 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:02338b286f8eac7f]…\nconsumers already obtain their\nvalue from WB. The standalone ALU must still present the exact signed\nresult for the entire valid response, including under backpressure.\n\nA scratch prototype passed the ALU and pipeline suites, including directed\ncases at all five scaling thresholds. It matched the ISS on four programs\nwith and without stalls, and completed ten CRC-validated CoreMark iterations\nin 3,140,795 marker cycles. An earlier version sharing the setup mux with the\ntrial array reached 84.240 MHz on placement option 1. The path from phase\ncontrol to the remainder register was within 0.010 ns of worst slack.\nRemoving that mux targets the remaining divider limit. Load and redirect\npaths can still limit the median below the 87.29 MHz replacement threshold.\n\nhyp-20261123-003-r12s2 [80.311, 82.178, 82.178] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_30_s1', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_26_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.452, 'arrival_ns': 17.746, 'logic_cells': 42, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} 6728\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
