{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_87","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r13s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-001-r13s0\ntitle: Estimate the divide quotient with a reciprocal ROM and correct it once\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median placement runs from quotient_q through the last\n  radix-16 trial into the shared EX/MEM ALU result. Narrowing the trial\n  subtractors reached 85.484 MHz in round 12. At 3,140,795 marker cycles,\n  replacement requires just over 87.29 MHz. CoreMark executes its divides\n  outside the markers, so the existing seven internal clocks are available\n  for pipelined ROM and DSP arithmetic.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the fifteen parallel restoring trials with a table estimate and\n  one exact correction. For unsigned magnitudes N and D >= 2, set\n  s = max(0, floor(log2(D)) - 10), k = D >> s, and take the first eight\n  fractional bits u of D / 2^s (zero-pad on the right). Store A[k] =\n  ceil(2^34 / k) in a synchronous 2048-row ROM. For k >= 1024 also store\n  H[k] = A[k] - A[k+1], using A[2048] for the last slope. Smaller rows\n  take u = 0. Form M = A[k] - ((u * H[k]) >> 8) and estimate\n  q = (N * M) >> (34 + s), retaining full product precision before shifting.\n\n  Linear interpolation of the convex reciprocal rounds upward with these\n  coefficients. Its contribution to quotient error is bounded by\n  N / (4 * 2^s * k^3). At s >= 1, k >= 1024, this is below 0.5.\n  Rounding both the ROM endpoints and the interpolated value upward adds\n  less than 2*N / 2^(34+s). Fraction truncation is exact for s <= 8\n  and costs less than N / (256 * 2^s * k^2) otherwise. The combined\n  error stays below one. For s = 0 only the ROM endpoint rounding applies.\n  Thus q is exact or one too large. Compute P = q*D using its low 33 bits\n  (P <= N+D). If P > N, decrement q and return (N-P)+D for REM, otherwise\n  use q and N-P. Handle D=0 and D=1 explicitly and restore the RV32M sign.\n\n  Pipeline normalization, ROM read, interpolation, quotient product and\n  correction across the existing handshake interval. Register each wide\n  product separately from the downstream shift or subtraction. Capture the\n  divide response in a dedicated EX/MEM data register for selection in MEM.\n  The ordinary EX forwarding path then carries integer and PC results only.\n  Keep request-latched ALTOPS results, response stability under stalls,\n  and the current divide consumer interlock. The test is whether eliminating\n  the trial array raises median Gowin Fmax above 87.29 MHz at the same\n  marker count. The other placement's load-verification path limits confidence.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Implement the staged reciprocal ROM, interpolation, two registered products and exact correction with the existing division handshake.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Add a dedicated magnitude field to the EX/MEM payload for a completed divide.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the divide magnitude separately at response transfer and remove its input from the fast result OR.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Select the dedicated DIV/REM magnitude for writeback and apply its registered sign.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: Exercise both correction outcomes, adjacent reciprocal buckets, powers of two, signed extremes and held responses against exact integer arithmetic.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
