 trial subtractions in one clock and has a\\nreported arrival time of 37.434 ns. The prior CoreMark trace retired zero\\nDIV or REM instructions inside the timing markers, so spending three extra\\nclocks per divide should buy Fmax at little cost to measured cycles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion's worst reported Gowin path starts and ends at remainder_q[1].\\nIt crosses eight\n…[1134 of 4206 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:074c11df0716e2f3]…\n raised Fmax by 8.66% on the earlier\\ncore. Moving the DSP across the EX/MEM boundary also separates the\\noperand forwarding muxes from the multiplication.\\n\\nOn the current champion, a scratch implementation reached 56.028,\\n60.916 and 60.916 MHz with Gowin's three placement options. Ten\\niterations with both bus stalls\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Execute multiplies in MEM from registered operands\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion runs ten CoreMark iterations in 4,202,272 marker cycles at\\na median Fmax of 50.042 MHz. Its worst path crosses the register file and EX ALU\\non the way to the data RAM address. The four multiply operations share\\nthe EX result mux with load and store addresses. A prior trace counted\\n93,960 MULs among 2,922,015 instructions between the timing markers.\\nThis makes a one-cycle penalty on immediate consumers affordable if a\\nregister boundary cuts the multiplier out of address generation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion runs ten \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
