{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r5s0','r3s0','r4s0','r5s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r.get('hypothesis'),r.get('changes'),r.get('perturbations'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 {'motivation': \"The champion's worst path on Gowin placement option 0 runs from the\\nregister file through the multiplier DSP and EX result mux to a data\\nRAM address pin, with a reported arrival time of 27.311 ns. This path\\nexists because dmem_addr takes the general EX/MEM alu_result. Every\\nRV32IM load and store needs only forwarded rs1 plus its immediate.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Register the full 32-bit sum rs1 + in.imm alongside the general result\\nat EX/MEM. This sum is already used for JALR in EX. Driving the memory\\naddress and byte-lane logic from that register removes the DSP and the\\ngeneral result mux from the RAM address path without adding a cycle.\\n\\nA scratch implementation reached 48.319, 49.590 and 49.590 MHz across\\nthe three Gowin placements, up from the champion's 45.640 MHz median.\\nWith both buses stalled pseudo-randomly, ten CoreMark iterations passed\\nCRC validation in 4,491,485 bracketed cycles, matching the champion.\\nTogether these measurements give 110.41 iterations/s, an 8.66% gain\\nover 101.61. Placement option 0 used 636 registers, up from 604.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 9, 'confidence': 'high'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'description': 'Add a 32-bit effective_addr field immediately after alu_result in ex_mem_t.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Register post-forwarding rs1 plus in.imm directly into effective_addr\\non the normal EX/MEM advance edge. Hold it with the memory controls\\nand store data during dmem backpressure.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use effective_a