{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nds=[json.loads(l) for l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor d in ds:\n if d['id'].endswith(('r2s1','r2s0','r6s2')):\n  print(d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': \"The champion's worst reported Gowin path starts and ends at remainder_q[1].\\nIt crosses eight dependent trial subtractions in one clock and has a\\nreported arrival time of 37.434 ns. The prior CoreMark trace retired zero\\nDIV or REM instructions inside the timing markers, so spending three extra\\nclocks per divide should buy Fmax at little cost to measured cycles.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Run the existing restoring divider for seven rounds of five quotient bits.\\nLoad a 35-bit shift register with three leading zeros and the 32-bit\\ndividend magnitude. Each round consumes five bits from the top, appends\\nfive quotient bits at the bottom and registers the partial remainder.\\nAfter 35 steps the low 32 quotient bits and the remainder give the RV32M\\nresults. This cuts the chain of subtract-and-select decisions from eight\\nto five while retaining the divider's current request and response ports.\\n\\nA scratch implementation reached 42.537, 43.681 and 43.681 MHz across\\nthe three Gowin placement options. The worst paths now end at the ID/EX\\noperand registers. Stalled CoreMark passed CRC validation and used\\n4,491,485 marker cycles for ten iterations, matching the champion.\\nThe 43.681 MHz median therefore gives 97.25 iterations/s, up 37.1%\\nfrom 70.94. The three extra clocks apply to each DIV/REM on workloads\\nthat use these instructions.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 37, 'confidence': 'high'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Zero-extend the 32-bit dividend magnitude into quotient_q[34:0].\\nShift from bit 34 and perform five existing 33-bit trial subtractions\\nper active clock. Select quotient_q[31:0] for final quotient sign\\ncorrection. Widen the existing one-hot round token to seven bits\\nin both real and ALTOPS builds. Assert\\nresponse-valid on the final update and hold the result until accepted.\\nKeep the existing signed remainder, zero-divisor and overflow handling.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Check for the first response exactly seven clocks after \n…[6881 of 9953 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ceaaa3e00360ab02]…\neffects on replay and gate divide requests independently of divide wait.'}, {'file': 'rtl/mem_stage.sv', 'description': 'Implement the synchronous 256-by-42-bit prediction table and validity vector. Verify each lookup, select the raw word for WB and RVFI, and train or invalidate it only on accepted accesses.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Allow issue behind LW, retain the subword and multiply interlocks, qualify bus holds using verified availability, and wire the predicted word and dependent-miss replay.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'During a replay hold, preserve the consumer and capture any matching WB operand before the completing load takes the WB slot.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Assert load-to-consumer retirement gaps of two cycles cold and one cycle warm in the successor replay test, with two cycles for every multiply-use pair. Check wrong-row carries, partial stores, aliases, MMIO, subword formatting and a dependent branch during backpressure.'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
