{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"micro_opt\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor line in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d.keys() if not d.get(\\\"id\\\") else d[\\\"id\\\"])\n h=d.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}); print(h if isinstance(h,str) else (h.get(\\\"title\\\"),h.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"))); print(str(d.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\"))[:3200])'\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-c4b728b\n(None, None)\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r1s0\n(None, \"Fold the four RV32M divide/remainder operations onto one unsigned restoring\\ndivider in EX. Capture the forwarded operands at issue and work on their\\nmagnitudes. Each cycle, perform eight shift-and-trial-subtract steps, using\\neach subtraction's borrow to select the remainder and emit one quotient bit.\\nRegister the intermediate state and reuse the same logic for four cycles.\\nSelect and sign-correct the requested result once. This bounds the arithmetic\\ndepth per clock and charges the extra cycles only to DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU.\\nAt the baseline's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles for ten iterations, a median\\nGowin Fmax of 8.17 MHz would deliver the projected 50% gain. Measure both\\nquantities because extra cycles before the start marker can shift the phase\\nof the pseudo-random bus stalls.\\n\")\n# Four-cycle restoring divider\n\nThe bench core scored **69.24 CoreMark iterations/s**, up from 12.12. Median Gowin Fmax rose from 5.442 MHz to **31.097 MHz**. Ten iterations took **4,491,485 bracketed cycles**, matching the supplied baseline.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 30.723 |\n| 1 | 31.097 |\n| 2 | 31.097 |\n\nThe divider captures the forwarded operand magnitudes on issue. Each of the next four clocks computes eight quotient bits through a shared chain of trial subtractions. The final quotient or remainder is selected and sign-corrected from registers and held until EX/MEM accepts it. A zero divisor naturally produces an all-ones quotient and the dividend magnitude as remainder. The issue logic suppresses quotient negation in that case. The same magnitude arithmetic gives the specified signed overflow result for INT_MIN divided by −1.\n\nEX also saves the original forwarded operands for channel-0 RVFI. A stalled memory request holds EX/MEM and delays issue. Once it advances, EX writes bubbles with zero controls while the divider runs. The PC and ID/EX can advance on the result-transfer edge, allowing an immediate consumer to use \n…[6741 of 9813 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7cdf1c9f37d3fabc]…\nfts supply the\\nother multiples.\\n\\nEach of six rounds resolves one radix-4 digit followed by one radix-8\\ndigit. For radix 8 bring down three bits and test D through 7D in\\nparallel. The borrow flags select the largest multiple that fits and\\nthe corresponding residual. For radix 4 select with a balanced borrow\\ntree. For radix 8 mask each residual by its winning interval and OR\\nthe eight masked words. Encode the quotient bits from the borrow flags\\nin parallel. This reduces the full-width feedback chain from three\\nserial subtract/select stages to two at the original latency.\\n\\nThe prototype passed the extended ALU vectors and matched the ISS on\\nselftest and all three random traces with both buses stalled. It completed\\nten CRC-validated iterations in 3,207,017 marker cycles. Gowin measured\\n66.252, 69.004 and 69.004 MHz, giving 215.17 iterations/s. The 4.48%\\ngain falls just short of the 4.6% replacement threshold. The median\\nplacement's worst path now runs from the word table through the EX ALU.\\n\")\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
