{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['round_id']>=9:\n  print(r['id'],r['critical_path'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r9s0 {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/words_words_0_1_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/predicted_pc_q_16_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.044, 'arrival_ns': 18.338, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']} Split EX result selection across the EX/MEM edge. Register one-hot result\ncontrols in ID. In EX compute rs1 + rs2 and rs1 + imm in parallel. Mask\nthe first sum for ADD and the second for ADDI, then OR them into one\n32-bit register. Reuse the effective-address sum for the immediate term.\nA second register captures the OR of the individually enabled SUB, Boolean,\ncomparison, shift, LUI, AUIPC, jump-link and completed divide results.\nFeed the bitwise OR of the two registers to EX/MEM forwarding and to MEM.\nThe ADD carry chain now ends at its own register after a two-term select.\n\nExclude jumps and AUIPC from the ordinary ADD enables. A divide result is\nconsumed only on response transfer. Qualify each use with the existing\nproducer controls and pipeline validity. The two result registers can\nsample every clock because the only EX/MEM holds are external memory\ntransactions. A held store consumes its registered address and store data,\nand a held load supplies its value through the separate load bypass or WB.\nThe replay and divide bubbles suppress register writes as before.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten CRC-validated iterations in\n3,207,017 bracketed cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 75.044,\n76.687 and 76.687 MHz, yielding 239.12 iterations/s. This is a 3.4%\ngain over the champion. The 4.6% replacement margin requires a median\nabove 77.574 MHz at this cycle count. The median placement's longest\npath now lies inside the divider.\n\nhyp-20261123-002-r9s1 {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/words_words_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_9_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.201, 'arrival_ns': 18.495, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} Move operand collection to the ID/EX boundary. Read the incoming instruction's\ntwo register indices from coherent\n…[8658 of 11730 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6912a7efa46b35c9]…\nexisting registered correction_q. This works even\nwhen target-range or alignment checks forced a fallthrough despite a taken\ntable prediction. Set the new strength when the sampled table direction\nmatched the recovered outcome. A formerly strong entry keeps its direction,\nand a formerly weak entry takes the recovered outcome. The existing registered\ntrain enable suppresses writes for reset, replay, holds, jumps and FENCE.I.\nBypass the pending direction and strength writes to fetch independently using\ntheir respective index widths. This removes counter-update logic from the\nforwarded-operand path and preserves one update per advancing branch.\n\nIn scratch cosim the split table with ordinary EX outcome training finished\nin 3,126,803 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all five CRCs correct,\nsaving 13,992 cycles. At that cycle count the 4.6 percent replacement margin\nrequires a median above 81.62 MHz. The clock gain depends on removing the\ntraining path without lengthening the fetch-side direction bypass.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
