{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"agent_message","text":"The median placement also has a tied path through the divider’s final trial. A divisor-magnitude split can shorten that arithmetic without adding cycles. The scratch divider passed the existing ALU suite, including held responses and divide-by-zero cases. I’ll use it for the proposal and check its routed timing."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_86","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r11s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261123-002-r11s1\ntitle: Split division at the 16-bit divisor boundary\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  In the champion's 83.455 MHz median placement, a path from quotient_q[30]\n  through the final divider trial to EX/MEM's ALU result ties the worst\n  setup slack at -6.982 ns. Every digit currently tests fifteen 36-bit\n  multiples. RV32 arithmetic bounds either the running remainder or the\n  final quotient to 16 bits, depending only on the divisor magnitude.\nhypothesis: |\n  Classify the unsigned divisor magnitude D at request acceptance. For\n  0 < D < 65536, the restored remainder is always below 65536. Appending\n  four bits gives a partial dividend below 2\"'^20, and 15D also fits in\n  20 bits. Use a 20-bit radix-16 array with a separate borrow bit per\n  subtraction, eight quotient digits and a 16-bit remainder register.\n\n  For D >= 65536, the high half of the dividend is already below D and\n  the quotient fits in 16 bits. Start with that high half as the remainder\n  and consume the low half two bits per digit. The full-width radix-4\n  array needs three trial multiples. In setup resolve the first digit from\n  dividend[31:14], using an 18-bit partial and explicit overflow detection,\n  while registering 3D for later rounds. Both classes then take six\n  registered rounds and one final trial on the held response state.\n\n  Select the class result with the operation captured at issue. Handle\n  zero explicitly with an all-ones quotient or the original dividend as\n  remainder. Preserve signed overflow, MEM sign correction, ALTOPS and\n  the request/response schedule under backpressure. The narrower trial\n  arrays should shorten carry paths and reduce routing demand. CoreMark\n  executes no divisions between markers, so the gain depends on Fmax.\n  At 3,140,795 marker cycles the 4.6 percent margin requires 87.30 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: Implement the two magnitude classes, narrow radix-16 trials, three full-width radix-4 trials, and the explicit zero result using the existing phase and response protocol.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: Exercise magnitudes 65535, 65536 and 65537, both signs, and dividends one below, at and above multiples. Alternate the two classes on consecutive requests and hold the response before switching.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json, yaml, jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r11s1.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('validated')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
