{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(l)\n if any(k in x['id'] for k in ['r12s2','r11s2','r14s0','r14s1','r14s2','r10s0']):\n  print(x['id']);print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis']);print(x.get('critical_path'));print(x.get('implementation_notes','')[:1000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-001-r10s0\nPipeline the existing table lookup by one bank-select bit without adding\na load-use cycle. Split the 256 payload rows into two 128-row distributed\nRAM banks. Read both with lookup_index[6:0]. At EX/MEM capture each bank's\nraw word, tag, signature and already selected word or extended subword.\nCapture lookup_index[7] with them. At each dependent EX operand form two\nenables from its existing speculative-load enable and that registered\nbank bit. OR the two masked candidates into the existing forwarding mux.\nThis places the bank decision in parallel with bypass selection. MEM uses\nthe same bank bit for verification, raw RVFI data and the WB candidate.\n\nStage the occupancy lookup at the same edge. Read a 16-bit group mask\nwith index[7:4] and capture it alongside its initialized flag and index.\nUse index[3:0] for the final bit select in MEM. Reset clears the 16 group\nflags and invalidates the candidate. Start the first update to each group\nfrom zero, set the target bit on install, and clear it on an accepted\nnarrow store. Suppress a same-row read/write collision and hold both\nbanks, their selects and their validity sample together during bus stalls.\nKeep full-address, alignment and format checks and the existing replay\nconditions so a wrong partial sum cannot authorize a bypass.\n\nThe directory-only control prototype passed the pipeline suite and the\ncanonical CoreMark run in 3,140,795 marker cycles. Its Gowin placements\nreached 77.475, 80.815 and 80.815 MHz, for 257.31 iterations/s. Eight of\nthe next timing paths after the divider in the median placement ran from\nWB load data to the table output registers. Splitting the payload targets\nthose paths. A median of 82.3 MHz at the measured cycle count would give\n262.04 iterations/s. The full forwarding design is a projection.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_mem/bank_value_q[0]_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_val_8_s1', 'slack_ns': -9.428, 'arrival_ns': 19.723, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id']}\n\nhyp-202611\n…[7235 of 10307 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:30c2f4ded6991168]…\nnd can accept its replacement on the same edge.\n\nFor each advancing lookup, reject the candidate if its index matches either\nthe logical update being captured or the older packet committing at the RAM\nport. This covers both a newly stale row and device-specific read-during-write\nbehavior. Use the existing miss, bus and dependent-replay paths on a conflict.\nRetain the candidate across holds. Reset clears occupancy and discards pending\nwork. Keep full tag and format verification, MMIO exclusion and store handshakes.\n\nThe prediction is that registering the write address, data and enables will\nimprove median Gowin Fmax with few extra marker cycles from collision misses.\nMeasure both against 88.718 MHz and 3,140,795 cycles. At the same cycle count\nthe 4.6% replacement gate requires a median above 92.80 MHz.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_14_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_fwd_rs1/sel.load_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.453, 'arrival_ns': 18.747, 'logic_cells': 27, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_fwd_rs1']}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
