{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_97","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r5s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-002-r5s1\ntitle: Resolve operand sources in decode and register the bypass matches\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,238,604 marker cycles for ten iterations at 61.962 MHz.\n  In Gowin placement 0, the three slowest paths launch from a register-file\n  BSRAM and end at a data RAM address. Two more start at the register file\n  and reach ID control and fetch PC within 0.1 ns of the worst arrival.\n  Moving the two read ports to distributed RAM alone reached 61.977 MHz.\n  Its new limiting path starts at a WB destination tag and passes through\n  the EX forwarding selector. Operand data and source selection both need\n  to reach the ID/EX boundary early.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retire the EX-stage register-number comparisons by resolving both source\n  tags one cycle earlier. In ID, read two asynchronous distributed-RAM\n  ports and compare each source against the EX and MEM destinations in\n  parallel. Register each operand and its two matches on the same ID/EX\n  advance. In EX a qualified match selects the corresponding registered\n  result, with the youngest writer taking priority. This splits operand\n  lookup from execution without a new pipeline stage or dependency bubble.\n\n  A scratch prototype passed eight selftest and random-program trace\n  comparisons, and passed the CoreMark UART and CRC checks in 4,238,604\n  marker cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 59.374, 65.269 and\n  65.269 MHz. The median gives 153.99 iterations/s, about 5.3% above\n  146.18. At this cycle count 64.82 MHz clears the 4.6% margin. The divider\n  feedback path now sets the measured clock limit.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Infer distributed RAM for two asynchronous reads with a common\n      synchronous write to both copies. Keep the data arrays reset-free.\n      Clear a per-register written mask on reset so every register reads\n      zero until first written. Give a matching WB write priority on each\n      port and suppress writes and bypass to x0.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the EX forwarding-unit instance with four ID comparisons of\n      rs1/rs2 against ID/EX.rd and EX/MEM.rd. Gate each with the producer's\n      reg_write and nonzero rd. Clock the four match bits when !stall_id,\n      alongside the existing operand capture. In EX qualify the stored hits\n      with the respective EX/MEM and MEM/WB reg_write controls, including\n      the retained WB payload on a dmem hold.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the two qualified match bits per operand as a priority mux. The\n      EX/MEM hit selects its result, then the MEM/WB hit selects WB data,\n      otherwise use the captured operand. Both hit bits may be set for\n      successive writers. Preserve the divide's request-time operand latch.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Write distinct values, reset after the writes and read old locations\n      through both ports. Check the first post-reset same-address write on\n      both ports, an untouched location and an attempted write to x0.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise simultaneous matches to successive writers of the same rd\n      with an immediate consumer behind a dmem hold. Include a faulting\n      younger writer whose suppressed write must expose the older result.\n      Check the consumer's channel-0 operands and result.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
