{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r7s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261123-002-r7s1\ntitle: Complete division in MEM with four quotient bits per clock\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin traces the champion's 20.904 ns, 102-cell setup path from\n  quotient_q[34] to remainder_q[9]. Three serial radix-4 digit selections\n  sit between those registers. CoreMark retires zero divides between its\n  timing markers. Letting a divide occupy MEM while it runs buys a clock\n  for a shallower recurrence within the existing formal progress bound.\nhypothesis: |\n  Launch DIV/REM from the forwarded operands as EX advances. Move the\n  instruction and its RVFI operands into EX/MEM on that same edge. MEM owns\n  the instruction until the divider response transfers directly into\n  MEM/WB. An immediately dependent instruction uses the existing WB-only\n  interlock. The ordinary EX result mux loses its divide-result input.\n\n  In the setup clock, compute 3D from the registered divisor magnitude D\n  and consume the top nibble N of the dividend magnitude. For D >= 16 the\n  prefix quotient is zero and the remainder is N. Otherwise read a\n  256-by-8 constant ROM addressed by {D[3:0], N}. Pack floor(N/D) in\n  the high nibble and N mod D in the low nibble. Row D=0 returns {15,N}.\n  Seed the 32-bit shift register with {dividend[27:0], prefix_quotient}\n  and zero-extend the prefix remainder. Seven clocks of two radix-4\n  digits each consume the remaining 28 bits exactly. Each digit tests\n  D, 2D and registered 3D in parallel with 35-bit borrow-producing\n  subtracts and selects the residual using the 2D borrow first.\n\n  A scratch implementation ran ten validated CoreMark iterations in\n  3,207,017 marker cycles. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 67.407,\n  69.512 and 69.512 MHz. The median gives 216.75 iter/s, 5.25% above\n  205.93. The margin over the 4.6% acceptance cutoff is narrow. The\n  slowest placement alone falls short. The median placement's worst\n  path now ends in the fetch successor table's occupancy bits.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the nibble ROM with elaboration-time constants, the setup\n      edge and seven four-bit rounds. Restore the quotient or remainder\n      sign using the captured operation. Keep divide-by-zero and signed\n      overflow exact. Use the same eight-clock response schedule under\n      ALTOPS. Hold the result under backpressure and allow a new request\n      on the edge that consumes a previous response. Reset cancels both.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Issue once on a valid advancing divide, gated by MEM hold and\n      load-miss replay. Register divide ownership alongside EX/MEM valid.\n      Use that bit and response-valid to request a MEM hold. Capture the\n      forwarded operands in the existing EX/MEM record for RVFI, and\n      route the completed result to MEM.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Treat a pending MEM divide like an outstanding data access. Hold\n      IF, ID/EX and EX/MEM together and use hold_mem_wb to drain the older\n      WB entry exactly once. Keep the separate load-miss replay behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect the divider result to MEM. Include all four DIV/REM opcodes\n      in the WB-only producer interlock with multiply and subword loads.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the completed divide result for the owning M opcode when\n      EX/MEM advances into WB. Use the existing valid and writeback path\n      for in-order retirement and forwarding.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Assert the eight-clock latency and request-ready behavior on\n      response turnover. Check a simultaneous response and new request,\n      all sixteen dividend prefixes with small and wide divisors, and\n      retain the signed, divide-by-zero, reset and backpressure vectors.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise adjacent independent divides on a response-turnover edge\n      and a dependent divide using WB forwarding. Check RVFI order and\n      both operands, including a divide launched after a stalled store.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r7s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint(h['title'],len(h['changes']))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
