{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor' and 'hypothesis' in d:\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][-850:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 if it is word-aligned and below 0x00100000. Otherwise predict PC+4.\nA pending register write can make a prediction stale, so EX resolves every\ntransfer using its forwarded operands, alignment checks and trap rules.\n\nWhen a valid control transfer advances through EX, compare its resolved\nsuccessor against the current registered fetch PC. That PC is the prediction\nselected at the edge that admitted this instruction to ID/EX. A mismatch\nredirects to the resolved successor and kills the younger instruction.\nA match lets ID accept the current fetch without a control bubble. Hold\nboth the PC and ID/EX during a data-memory freeze. On release, correction\ntakes priority even when imemReady is low. A load-use bubble or unready\ninstruction bus must never consume a prediction. EX supplies the resolved\npc_next to RVFI and PC+4 as the jump link value.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r2s2 ent or decrement with saturation. A count\nof zero frees the entry. Leave the occupant alone on a not-taken miss.\nWrite targets only when word-aligned and below 0x00100000. JALR learns the\nresolved target after bit zero is cleared.\n\nThe registered fetch PC is the successor actually selected for the\ninstruction in ID/EX. On an aligned taken transfer, compare it with the\nfull resolved target. For any other instruction that predicted taken,\ncompare it with PC+4. This also recovers from changed code, a stale JALR\ntarget, or a transfer that traps. Only a mismatch squashes the younger\ninstruction. Hold PC and metadata together during a data-memory or divide\nhold. A load-use bubble holds the fetch PC and clears ID/EX. Apply\n