cles stayed flat because CoreMark executes all five DIVU sites outside its timing markers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.82%): Predicting the next PC from decode operands and flushing only when EX resolved a different successor saved a fetch slot on corr\n…[7337 of 10409 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2cef94dc1052e744]…\ngnedness matched, with WB reusing the same verified value.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-001-r10s0 (regression, -1.03%): Selecting between two registered load-table banks in the EX bypass preserved CoreMark's 3,140,795 timed cycles, but the path from `bank_value_q` through subword verification and replay control to the ID operand register limited median Fmax to 77.566 MHz.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r10s1 (improvement, +6.48%): Using the response cycle for the final radix-16 digit let each divider round test 15 multiples in parallel, cutting the feedback path from two subtract/select stages to one, while deferring sign correction to MEM cost no cycles in CoreMark's divide-free timing window.\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-003-r10s2 (regression, +1.29%): Splitting 512 direction bits from 128 shared strength bits saved 13,992 timed CoreMark cycles, but training from `correction_q` left the path from the successor BRAM to `predicted_pc_q` limiting median Fmax to 79.029 MHz, below the 81.62 MHz needed for replacement.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
