{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|split direction|successor BRAM|load prediction' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-600\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict the next fetch PC from decode-stage register values\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline fetches PC+4 after every instruction. IF and ID share a cycle,\\nso each aligned taken branch or jump squashes one fetch slot when EX redirects.\\nBoth source registers are already read in that cycle, including same-cycle WB\\nbypass. When these values are current, they give the exact branch direction\\nor indirect jump target. Restricting prediction to those signals keeps the\\npredictor off the combinational EX ALU result path.\\n\\nA scratch\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Predict recurring control transfers with a 128-entry branch target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion fetches PC+4 and loses one fetch slot on each taken transfer.\\nIts timed CoreMark trace has about 358,000 taken branches and jumps in ten\\niterations. The previous decode-operand predictor saved 7.3% of cycles.\\nA branch target buffer (BTB) can recover most of those slots by reading\\nthe last resolved target and a direction counter directly from the fetch PC.\\n\\nA scratch prototype on the champion's four-round divider core \n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Predict direct branches with a 128-entry direction table\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"IF and ID share one cycle. Every taken transfer on the champion discards a\\nfetch slot when EX redirects. The previous 128-entry target buffer recovered\\n301,480 of 4,491,485 bracketed cycles on an older divider core. The current\\nchampion reaches 45.64 MHz. A direct transfer encodes its own target, so\\nlearning branch direction alone can recover a fetch slot with a one-bit\\ndecision in EX.\\n\\nA scratch prototype on the current champion cut ten CoreMar\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Predict the successor instruction from the preceding fetch PC\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion predicts direct branch targets accurately enough to save\\n289,213 cycles over ten C\n…[3539 of 6611 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:25e6a2c52408f31d]…\nlets an LW consumer execute immediately, but every\\nadjacent byte or halfword consumer waits for WB. Servicing all widths saved\\n81,075 of 3,207,017 marker cycles in round seven. Round eight's asymmetric\\nRAM prototype achieved the same cycle count, but its forwarding and WB\\npaths reconstructed the same value differently and the register consistency\\nproof timed out. The current median placement also puts the register\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261123-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Train a split direction predictor from the registered fetch correction\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion mispredicted 54,467 of 529,588 advancing conditional branches\\nduring the ten-iteration CoreMark bracket. Its 128 counters alias every 512\\nbytes of code. In a repeat of Gowin placement option 1, two of the five\\nlongest paths ran from the forwarded rs1 match to the direction-counter\\nupdate registers. The fetch stage already registers the correction that\\nidentifies whether the chosen successor was wrong.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
