{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(s)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r11s2','r7s2','r5s2','r8s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261123-003-r5s2 On each accepted fetch, use the current PC[10:2] to read a 512-entry\nsynchronous last-successor table. Each row carries the observed successor's\n18-bit word address, its 32-bit instruction, its 18-bit direct target word\naddress and two flags for a legal, aligned, in-range JAL or conditional\nbranch. Compute the flags and target from the live instruction and its PC\nwhen filling the row. The 70-bit payload fits two 36-bit-wide block RAM\nbanks at depth 512. Keep one resettable occupancy bit per row.\n\nOn a ready bus, issue the live word and use its direct-target calculation.\nDuring an imem stall, issue the registered candidate only if occupied and\nits reconstructed byte address equals the entire fetch PC. For that word,\nuse the stored direct target for JAL or when the current PC's direction\ncounter predicts a conditional branch taken. Otherwise select PC+4.\nDrive decode and the load/multiply-use interlock from the same selected\nword. Gate acceptance with bus-ready OR verified-hit so the address check\ncan run in parallel with decoding.\nThis moves target addition for a predicted word to the earlier fill cycle.\nA source-index collision is harmless because the successor PC is verified.\n\nTrain the preceding accepted PC's row only with an accepted, aligned,\nin-range live bus word. A predicted word may itself start the next lookup.\nKeep the candidate and predecessor across holds and retries. An advancing\nEX correction redirects the PC and squashes the younger slot even when\nits prediction hits or imemReady is low.\nKeep the preceding index through correction so the corrected fetch can\ntrain it. Suppress the new candidate on a same-row read/write collision.\nOn reset or an advancing FENCE.I, clear occupancy and pending metadata.\nFENCE.I squashes the younger fetch and restarts at its architectural PC+4,\nafter any older stalled store has advanced.\n\nhyp-20261123-003-r7s2 Split a 256-word load-value predictor into four 64-row distributed\nRAM banks. As in the champion, assemble the predicted word index from\nind\n…[5188 of 8260 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4097d8cdc5ba0389]…\nad's predicted-value bits [9:2]\nor the older non-load's EX/MEM ALU-result bits [9:2]. This source predicts\nzero carries and can be wrong after an older load miss or late M result.\nSelect the four row candidates with the registered rs1 bypass enables.\nThis removes the WB result mux and address arithmetic from the path\nbetween the WB register and the synchronous table read.\n\nHold the WB row with WB data and use the held consumer's displacement\nwhen decode stalls. MEM must match the saved index and tag against the\nfull forwarded effective address and retain every occupancy, collision,\nalignment and format check. Allocate and invalidate using the true\naddress. An incorrect estimate falls through to the existing bus and\ndependent-replay path.\n\nA scratch implementation ran ten CRC-validated iterations in 3,134,538\nbracketed cycles with both buses stalled, versus 3,140,795 for the\nchampion. It needs a median above 87.12 MHz to clear the replacement\nmargin. The fitness estimate depends primarily on that clock gain.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
