{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r6s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261123-003-r6s2\ntitle: Predict the load word index before the effective-address carry resolves\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion needs 3,463,993 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Its timed\n  retirement trace contains 544,852 loads. A trace replay with a 256-word\n  direct-mapped table, filled on loads and invalidated on stores, found that\n  short independent additions predicted the correct row for 526,591 loads.\n  Of these, 387,446 also found the right word. The next instruction's source\n  fields matched the load destination on 141,736 of those hits. This gives\n  the predictor two opportunities to save cycles on the same load, the\n  memory-ready wait and the dependent instruction's issue interlock.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the row of an ordinary RAM load in EX, while the existing ALU\n  computes its exact byte address. Use the forwarded base and I-immediate.\n  Form the eight row bits by independently adding bits [4:2] modulo 8 and\n  [9:5] modulo 32, concatenating the two sums in address order. Read a\n  synchronous 256-row table with this index at the EX/MEM edge. Each entry\n  supplies a previously observed raw 32-bit word and its RAM word address.\n  No carry from a lower field or full address addition precedes the read.\n\n  In MEM, qualify the registered prediction with validity, the full resolved\n  word address, access alignment and the ordinary RAM range below 1 MiB.\n  On a verified hit, complete irrespective of dmemReady and forward the\n  correctly extracted load result to a dependent instruction in EX. Let\n  that consumer issue speculatively one cycle after the load. On a miss,\n  perform the regular bus read and write back the response. If the younger\n  EX instruction needs that result, squash it before any side effect,\n  suppress its redirect, predictor training and divide request, and refetch\n  it at its own PC after the load completes. An independent younger\n  instruction can advance with the completing miss.\n\n  Fill only from accepted, aligned RAM reads. Invalidate the indexed\n  entry on each accepted in-range store, including partial stores.\n  Capture validity at lookup, suppress a candidate on a simultaneous\n  same-row write or invalidation, and hold it with a frozen EX/MEM slot.\n  Reset clears validity. MMIO, out-of-range accesses and misalignment use\n  the architectural path. RVFI reports the resolved address, mask and\n  raw memory word for both hit and miss retirements.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Generate the predicted row from short independent additions on forwarded load operands. Give a verified load value normal youngest-writer forwarding priority and kill all effects of an instruction selected for replay.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Add the synchronous tagged load-word table and occupancy bits. Check the predicted row against the exact EX/MEM address, manage fills and store invalidation, and select the verified or live raw word for load extraction and retirement.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Allow issue behind a load, freeze on an outstanding bus miss, and replay a dependent EX instruction once on miss completion. Preserve the multiply interlock and the existing holds for older work.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire the EX lookup, MEM verification and load bypass. Prioritize a load-miss replay over the younger instruction's own correction, including when instruction fetch is stalled.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check consecutive retirement of a warm load and its consumer with dmemReady low. Exercise cross-field carries, aliasing, partial stores, cold dependent branches and divides, prolonged backpressure and MMIO bypass.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
