r LW. In the producer's EX\n  cycle, predict an eight-bit word index from the forwarded base and\n  signed offset. Add bits [9:5] modulo 32 and bits [4:2] modulo 8.\n  Concatenate the sums and discard carry between fields. Read a 256-entry\n  synchronous table at this index while separately registering the exact\n  32-bit effective address. Each row holds a 32-bit raw RAM word and its\n  ten-bit tag [19:10], with one resettable occupancy bit per row.\n\n  In the following cycle, feed the candidate word directly into the\n  dependent instruction's EX operand mux. Give the prediction its own\n  one-hot input\n…[1993 of 5065 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:96f763c3c20f49da]…\nuppress all younger effects on replay and gate divide requests independently of divide wait.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: Implement the synchronous 256-by-42-bit prediction table and validity vector. Verify each lookup, select the raw word for WB and RVFI, and train or invalidate it only on accepted accesses.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Allow issue behind LW, retain the subword and multiply interlocks, qualify bus holds using verified availability, and wire the predicted word and dependent-miss replay.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: During a replay hold, preserve the consumer and capture any matching WB operand before the completing load takes the WB slot.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Assert load-to-consumer retirement gaps of two cycles cold and one cycle warm in the successor replay test, with two cycles for every multiply-use pair. Check wrong-row carries, partial stores, aliases, MMIO, subword formatting and a dependent branch during backpressure.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
