{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"tail -4 '/srv/hwebench/tmp/51daa901/7c2a8782/predictor-r8-ijgQ0rE3/accepted/pnr/gowin_p1/gw.log' && tail -2 '/srv/hwebench/tmp/51daa901/7c2a8782/predictor-r8-ijgQ0rE3/split/pnr/gowin_p1/gw.log' && date -u && cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r8s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"Running technical mapping ...\n[60%] Tech-Mapping Phase 0 completed\n[65%] Tech-Mapping Phase 1 completed\n[75%] Tech-Mapping Phase 2 completed\n[65%] Tech-Mapping Phase 1 completed\n[75%] Tech-Mapping Phase 2 completed\nMon Nov 23 01:57:54 UTC 2026\nid: hyp-20261123-003-r8s2\ntitle: \"Pipeline training and lookup of the fetch successor predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion runs ten timed CoreMark iterations in 3,207,017 cycles at\n  70.620 MHz. Its reported 19.918 ns path ends at the fetch successor RAM.\n  The other two round-seven designs report paths ending at the same\n  predictor's occupancy bits. Today a late EX redirect or replay influences\n  both RAM enables and the 512-row occupancy update through fetch acceptance.\n  The predictor already hides instruction-bus stalls. Its port timing is\n  the opportunity to raise throughput at the existing hit rate.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read the 512-row successor predictor at the current PC index every clock.\n  Register whether that lookup was accepted. After an accepted lookup use\n  its synchronous RAM output directly. On the first subsequent hold or\n  redirect retain that output in a shadow register, and use the retained\n  candidate through further holds. The shadow can capture on the previous\n  cycle's registered acceptance bit. Keep candidate validity tied to the\n  logical lookup and compare its full successor address with the actual PC\n  before issuing when imemReady is low.\n\n  Put a one-entry pipelined write port on predictor training. Capture the\n  preceding index and full 70-bit successor record with a valid bit for an\n  accepted live fill. On the following edge that registered transaction\n  writes both RAM and occupancy. Sustain one fill per clock. Suppress a\n  candidate on a physical same-row read/write collision and on a same-row\n  fill still in flight. Reset and advancing FENCE.I invalidate occupancy,\n  pending training and candidate validity together. An EX correction still\n  wins PC selection immediately. Verified cached instructions, live fetch\n  priority and the direction predictor retain their current semantics.\n\n  This takes same-cycle EX control off both physical predictor ports. At\n  the champion cycle count the 4.6% acceptance margin requires a median\n  clock above 73.87 MHz. Measure bracketed cycles as well as Gowin Fmax,\n  since suppressing a colliding fill can cost an issue slot.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Run the successor RAM read independently of acceptance, retain the last accepted candidate across holds, and register fill data and address before updating the RAM and occupancy. Qualify validity for collisions and FENCE.I.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Warm a successor, hold it across multiple stalled or redirected cycles with poisoned imem data, then check retirement. Exercise adjacent same-row fills and invalidate a pending fill with FENCE.I before fetching modified code.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
